add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Sinus Bradycardia and the Indication for Permanent Pacing',
 'A 66-year-old has sinus rhythm at 44/min on a routine ECG, normal PR and QRS intervals, normal thyroid testing and no rate-slowing medication. There is no syncope, presyncope or exercise limitation; pulse increases appropriately during walking. Which next approach is most appropriate?',
 'Clinical follow-up without pacemaker implantation at present',[
 ('Dual-chamber pacemaker implantation','Dual-chamber pacing can relieve symptoms attributable to sinus node dysfunction. This patient has no attributable symptoms or chronotropic limitation; the resting rate alone does not establish a pacing indication.'),
 ('Electrophysiology study to measure sinus node recovery time','Invasive sinus node assessment may contribute when an electrophysiology study is being performed for another relevant indication. It is not the routine next test for an asymptomatic isolated slow sinus rate with reassuring clinical assessment.'),
 ('Implantable loop recorder insertion','Long-term recording can establish a symptom-rhythm relationship in infrequent unexplained syncope. There are no unexplained events here, so an invasive recorder does not answer a current consequential diagnostic question.')],
 [S('Symptoms and Context Determine Significance','**A slow sinus rate is a descriptive finding, not a sufficient diagnosis of clinically important sinus node dysfunction.** Assess symptoms, activity, medications, autonomic influences and systemic contributors.',
    'The US 2018 framework uses sinus rate below 50/min and pauses longer than three seconds as potential descriptive components, but neither supplies a stand-alone permanent-pacing threshold.',
    'The decision is different for advanced nonreversible AV block, where prognostic pacing can be appropriate without symptoms. Do not transfer that rule to isolated sinus bradycardia.'),
  S('Select Proportionate Evaluation','**Establish whether bradycardia explains a clinical problem before choosing a device.** Ask about fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, dizziness, syncope and timing; review ECG conduction and reversible causes.',
    'Ambulatory recording is useful when intermittent symptoms need correlation. Exercise testing is appropriate when chronotropic limitation is suspected, rather than assuming that a low resting pulse predicts an inadequate exercise response.',
    'This patient has no current symptom-driven indication for pacing. Explain follow-up and reassessment if new exertional limitation or transient loss of consciousness develops. Pacing for sinus node disease generally aims to improve attributable symptoms and quality of life; exposing an asymptomatic patient to implantation, lead and infection risks requires a separate defensible indication.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Sleep-Related Sinus Pauses and Obstructive Sleep Apnea',
 'A patient monitored for palpitations has sinus pauses of 4.2–5.0 seconds occurring exclusively during sleep. Daytime rhythm and exercise tolerance are normal, and there is no syncope. The partner reports loud snoring and witnessed apneas. Which next step is most appropriate?',
 'Evaluate and treat sleep apnea while reassessing the rhythm clinically',[
 ('Implant a permanent pacemaker based on the longest nocturnal pause','Pacing can be appropriate when another qualifying indication exists, but sleep-related sinus pauses alone do not establish it. The pause duration must not be detached from the exclusively nocturnal, asymptomatic context.'),
 ('Perform a sinus node electrophysiology study before sleep evaluation','Invasive testing may be considered in selected unresolved cases, often during a study for another indication. The supplied apnea phenotype provides a more direct, potentially reversible explanation and should be evaluated first.'),
 ('Prescribe a daytime chronotropic drug to prevent the nocturnal pauses','Chronotropic medicines have selected specialist uses but are not standard treatment for apnea-associated pauses in an asymptomatic daytime patient. They do not correct upper-airway obstruction and can introduce tachyarrhythmia or other adverse effects.')],
 [S('Identify a Treatable Nocturnal Trigger','**Sleep-disordered breathing can produce substantial nocturnal bradyarrhythmia through hypoxemia and autonomic fluctuation.** Snoring and witnessed apneas warrant formal sleep assessment rather than assuming intrinsic sinus node failure.',
    'Document whether events are confined to sleep and whether symptoms or high-grade conduction abnormalities occur while awake. Review medications and other reversible contributors in parallel.',
    'A pause detected by a monitor is not itself a symptom. Confirm the recording and interpret it alongside the history rather than applying a generic three-second boundary as an implantation rule.'),
  S('Treat the Cause and Reassess','**The US 2018 guideline advises against permanent pacing for sleep-related sinus bradycardia or transient pauses unless another pacing indication is present.** Appropriate sleep-apnea treatment can reduce associated rhythm disturbances.',
    'Select sleep therapy from the confirmed apnea phenotype and relevant contraindications; optimize weight, alcohol/sedative exposure and associated cardiovascular disease where appropriate.',
    'Reassess if daytime symptoms, awake pauses, progressive conduction disease or persistent unexplained events emerge. Treating apnea does not justify ignoring a separate nonreversible high-grade AV block, but the vignette supplies no such indication. Clarify that the current plan addresses a reversible mechanism while avoiding an unnecessary implanted device; follow-up should assess clinical response rather than merely whether one overnight tracing appears improved.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

R['EPDEFS2006']=('ACC/AHA/HRS electrophysiology data definitions, 2006','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2006.09.020')
add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Second-Degree Sinoatrial Exit Block',
 'A high-quality rhythm strip shows identical sinus P waves with a stable P–P interval of 800 ms. Repeated pauses between visible P waves measure 1600 ms, with no intervening premature atrial activity. Conducted beats have unchanged PR intervals. Which mechanism is most consistent with this pattern?',
 'Second-degree type II sinoatrial exit block',[
 ('Sinus arrest','Failure of sinus impulse formation produces a pause whose duration need not preserve the previous sinus cycle. A reproducible exact multiple of a stable atrial cycle instead supports exit block, although surface ECG inference is not absolute.'),
 ('Second-degree Mobitz II AV block','Mobitz II AV block causes nonconducted P waves while atrial activity continues. The pause here involves missing atrial depolarization rather than visible P waves failing to conduct to the ventricles.'),
 ('A blocked premature atrial complex','A premature atrial depolarization can be hidden in the preceding T wave and produce an apparent pause. Careful recording here excludes intervening premature activity and shows repeated preservation of the underlying sinus-cycle timing.')],
 [S('Use Atrial Timing Rather Than the Ventricular Pause Alone','**Sinoatrial exit block is failure of a sinus impulse to activate adjacent atrial tissue.** Sinus node activity is not directly visible on the surface ECG; the mechanism is inferred from the atrial timing pattern.',
    'With a stable baseline cycle, a type II pattern produces a missing P–QRS–T sequence and a pause that is an integral multiple of the preceding P–P interval. Here 1600 ms equals two cycles of 800 ms.',
    'Sinus arrest concerns impulse formation rather than exit conduction. Coincidental timing, sinus arrhythmia and concealed atrial ectopy limit certainty, so a compatible pattern is more defensible than claiming direct proof of sinus-node depolarization.'),
  S('Distinguish Related Conduction Patterns','**Inspect P waves, including the preceding T waves, before labeling a pause.** A blocked atrial premature beat can mimic sinus node disease, while AV block retains atrial depolarizations during ventricular pauses.',
    'Sinoatrial Wenckebach typically shows grouped atrial beating with shortening P–P intervals before the pause. First-degree sinoatrial delay cannot be diagnosed reliably from an ordinary surface strip.',
    'Clinical management depends on attributable symptoms, reversible causes and the broader rhythm evaluation, not the exit-block label alone. Review rate-slowing drugs, autonomic triggers and systemic disease. Symptomatic recurrent pauses require monitored assessment and consideration of pacing after the mechanism and indication are established. A technically uncertain strip should prompt better recording rather than an irreversible treatment decision from one apparent gap.')],['EPDEFS2006','US2018'],
 table=(['Pause pattern','Useful distinction'],[
 ['Type II SA exit block','Stable P–P cycle; missing atrial cycle; pause an integral multiple'],
 ['Sinus arrest','Pause not required to preserve baseline cycle'],
 ['AV block','P waves continue during absent ventricular responses'],
 ['Blocked atrial ectopy','Premature abnormal P may distort the preceding T wave']]))

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Tachycardia–Bradycardia Syndrome',
 'A patient has recurrent presyncope immediately after palpitations stop. Ambulatory ECG shows paroxysmal AF terminating spontaneously, followed by 6.2 seconds without atrial activity before sinus rhythm resumes. Symptoms occur during this interval. The patient takes no nodal-blocking medication. Which diagnosis best explains the episodes?',
 'Tachycardia–bradycardia syndrome with a symptomatic postconversion sinus pause',[
 ('Paroxysmal high-grade AV block','AV block would leave atrial depolarizations that fail to conduct during the ventricular pause. Here the absent atrial activity after AF termination identifies a sinus recovery problem rather than continued atrial activity with failed AV conduction.'),
 ('Post-tachycardia vagally mediated AV block','Vagal AV block can accompany sinus slowing and nonconducted P waves in a compatible clinical setting. The recording instead shows an atrial pause after AF termination, supporting a tachy-brady manifestation of sinus node dysfunction.'),
 ('AF with a slow ventricular response','Slow ventricular response during ongoing AF retains fibrillatory atrial activity. The symptomatic pause follows termination of AF and precedes resumption of sinus activity, so it is not simply slow conduction during persistent AF.')],
 [S('Recognize the Postconversion Mechanism','**Tachycardia–bradycardia syndrome combines atrial tachyarrhythmia with bradycardic manifestations of sinus node dysfunction.** Symptoms may be most prominent during the pause after AF, flutter or atrial tachycardia terminates.',
    'Atrial disease and impaired sinus automaticity can coexist; suppression during tachycardia contributes to delayed sinus recovery. The observed symptom–rhythm correlation is more consequential than a pause duration considered without context.',
    'This is not necessarily medication toxicity. Review drugs and reversible causes even when the first recording is obtained off rate-slowing treatment, and distinguish an atrial pause from ventricular asystole despite continuing P waves.'),
  S('Treat Both the Symptomatic Pause and the Atrial Arrhythmia','**A symptomatic prolonged pause warrants prompt specialist assessment of pacing and rhythm-control options.** Acute recurrent unstable events require monitored stabilization rather than routine outpatient observation.',
    'Pacing can prevent bradycardic symptoms and enable otherwise necessary treatment when appropriate. In selected AF-associated presentations, successful ablation can reduce pauses and may avoid a pacemaker; candidacy depends on the overall sinus-node and atrial substrate.',
    'A pacemaker does not abolish AF or remove stroke risk. Assess anticoagulation independently and discuss the expected benefit and burden of each strategy. Starting a rate-slowing drug without addressing clinically important pauses can aggravate the bradycardic phase. Conversely, a single postconversion pause should be interpreted with symptoms, reversibility and recurrence rather than treated as an automatic device rule for every patient.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Chronotropic Incompetence: Exercise Assessment',
 'A patient has reproducible exertional fatigue, sinus rate 56/min and normal resting AV conduction. Echocardiography and hemoglobin are unremarkable, and no rate-slowing medication is taken. Which investigation most directly evaluates whether the heart-rate response contributes to the symptoms?',
 'Symptom-limited exercise ECG with assessment of effort and heart-rate response',[
 ('Twenty-four-hour ambulatory ECG during usual sedentary activity','Ambulatory recording can correlate intermittent symptoms and rhythm, especially when a symptom/activity diary is available. A sedentary recording may not impose the workload needed to assess the supplied exertional limitation as directly as supervised exercise.'),
 ('Electrophysiology study measuring corrected sinus node recovery time','Invasive sinus node measures assess aspects of automaticity but are not the preferred first investigation of an inadequate exercise-rate response. Selected invasive testing does not replace evaluating the actual symptomatic workload.'),
 ('Resting atropine challenge as the principal diagnostic test','An atropine response can supply information about autonomic influences in selected assessments, but does not reproduce physiological exercise demand or establish whether rate behavior explains the patient’s exertional symptoms.')],
 [S('Measure Rate Reserve in the Correct Context','**Chronotropic incompetence is an inadequate heart-rate increase for metabolic demand.** A resting sinus rate alone cannot establish the diagnosis; assess symptoms, achieved workload and the quality of exercise effort.',
    'A commonly used criterion is failure to achieve ==80% of expected heart-rate reserve==. The achieved fraction is (peak rate − resting rate)/(predicted maximal rate − resting rate); this differs from simply taking 80% of the predicted maximum.',
    'Age-prediction equations are estimates. Beta blockers, comorbidity, exercise protocol and submaximal effort alter interpretation, so one mathematical boundary does not replace individualized assessment.'),
  S('Connect the Result to a Treatment Decision','**Exercise ECG documents rate, rhythm, conduction and symptoms together.** It can also reveal exercise-related AV block, ischemic changes or a non-rate explanation for stopping.',
    'If exertional symptoms correlate with an inadequate sinus response after reversible causes are addressed, specialist pacing assessment may be appropriate. Rate-responsive programming is selected to meet a demonstrated clinical need rather than to normalize a resting number.',
    'A low peak pulse after a poor effort or a test limited by orthopedic pain does not prove intrinsic sinus node disease. When uncertainty remains, a fuller cardiopulmonary exercise assessment or activity-linked monitoring can help separate circulatory, pulmonary and deconditioning contributors. Discuss expected symptom benefit and procedural burden before choosing a device; chronotropic limitation should not be treated as proof that pacing will correct every source of exertional fatigue.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Medication-Associated Sinus Bradycardia',
 'A patient develops fatigue and lightheadedness after atenolol is increased for uncomplicated hypertension. ECG shows sinus rhythm at 42/min with normal PR and QRS intervals. BP and perfusion are stable; electrolytes and thyroid tests are normal. There is no coronary, HF or arrhythmic indication for beta blockade. Which initial strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Reduce or withdraw atenolol and substitute a suitable non-rate-slowing antihypertensive with reassessment',[
 ('Implant a pacemaker to permit continuation of the current atenolol dose','Pacing to permit necessary treatment may be appropriate when symptomatic bradycardia has no reasonable therapeutic alternative. Atenolol here is used for hypertension with several non-rate-slowing alternatives, so correcting the drug trigger comes first.'),
 ('Replace atenolol with verapamil and reassess the pulse','Verapamil can treat selected hypertension or arrhythmia settings, but also suppresses sinus and AV nodal function. It does not reliably remove the negative chronotropic mechanism responsible for the new symptoms.'),
 ('Begin an oral chronotropic agent while retaining atenolol','Selected specialist chronotropic treatment may be considered when pacing is unsuitable or a particular temporary indication exists. Counteracting a dispensable rate-slowing drug with another medicine adds treatment burden instead of removing the identified cause.')],
 [S('Correct the Extrinsic Cause First','**Medication exposure can produce or uncover symptomatic sinus bradycardia.** The temporal relation to a dose increase, reassuring conduction pattern and absence of other abnormalities support an initial drug-focused assessment.',
    'Review beta blockers, verapamil/diltiazem, digoxin, antiarrhythmics and other relevant prescriptions or interactions. Hypothyroidism, hypothermia, hypoxia and metabolic disturbances also warrant consideration according to the clinical presentation.',
    'A drug association does not exclude underlying disease. Reassess symptoms and rhythm after dose modification rather than assuming either permanent intrinsic dysfunction or guaranteed complete reversibility.'),
  S('Distinguish Optional From Necessary Treatment','**The indication for the offending medicine changes the pacing decision.** Non-rate-slowing blood-pressure therapy can replace a beta blocker used solely for uncomplicated hypertension when suitable.',
    'In contrast, essential treatment without an acceptable alternative may justify pacing assessment when symptoms are attributable to bradycardia. That separate decision requires balancing the benefit of the medicine and the device.',
    'Plan medication changes according to dose, duration and withdrawal risks; avoid an unexamined abrupt cessation in a patient with a different compelling indication. Arrange pulse, pressure and symptom review. Persistent symptoms despite recovery of rate should prompt a broader evaluation, while persistent bradycardia after drug withdrawal may reveal intrinsic sinus node disease. Hemodynamic instability, syncope with dangerous rhythm or high-grade AV block would require a more urgent monitored pathway than this stable vignette.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Pacing to Permit Clinically Necessary Treatment',
 'A patient has recurrent presyncope documented during sinus bradycardia on clinically necessary guideline-directed treatment. Specialist review finds no acceptable alternative regimen; reducing treatment repeatedly causes recurrence of the condition it controls. Other reversible causes have been addressed. Which long-term approach best matches the US 2018 bradycardia guideline?',
 'Offer permanent pacing to relieve attributable bradycardic symptoms and permit necessary treatment',[
 ('Withdraw the necessary treatment and use symptom surveillance','Drug withdrawal is appropriate when treatment is dispensable or an acceptable alternative exists. The stem establishes repeated clinical need without a reasonable substitute, so withdrawal sacrifices a necessary benefit rather than resolving the complete problem.'),
 ('Retain the regimen and insert a loop recorder before making a pacing decision','A loop recorder is useful when infrequent unexplained symptoms lack rhythm documentation. Presyncope is already correlated with bradycardia here; further diagnostic waiting does not address the established symptomatic mechanism.'),
 ('Use chronic oral chronotropic therapy instead of device assessment','Oral chronotropic treatment has selected specialist roles when pacing is unsuitable or declined. It is not the guideline-preferred substitute for pacing in this documented necessary-treatment setting and may create additional rhythm or interaction problems.')],
 [S('Establish Both Symptom Attribution and Treatment Necessity','**The US 2018 guideline recommends pacing when clinically necessary guideline-directed treatment causes symptomatic sinus bradycardia and no alternative exists.** The goal is increased rate and relief of attributable symptoms.',
    'Confirm the symptom–rhythm relationship, exclude remediable contributors and review whether the medicine and dose remain necessary. A vague preference to retain a drug is insufficient to establish this indication.',
    'This differs from a beta blocker used only for uncomplicated hypertension, where substitution may avoid a device. The same apparent drug effect can lead to a different decision because its clinical necessity differs.'),
  S('Plan a Device Strategy With Realistic Goals','**Choose pacing after an individualized discussion of expected benefit and treatment burden.** Consider atrial rhythm, AV conduction, ventricular function and anticipated ventricular pacing when selecting the system.',
    'Pacing does not treat every adverse effect of the necessary medicine. Continue monitoring pressure, interactions, organ function and the underlying condition after implantation.',
    'Discuss procedural complications, infection, leads, follow-up and later generator replacement. The indication does not require inventing a minimum pause duration once clinically important symptoms are convincingly attributable to sinus bradycardia. If the medication can later be reduced or replaced, reassess the overall regimen, but do not assume that a pacemaker makes unrestricted dose escalation safe. Severe recurrent instability requires immediate monitored management while the durable strategy is arranged.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

R['TBSRCT2024']=('Randomized trial of ablation versus pacing in paroxysmal AF with tachy-brady syndrome, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1186/s12872-024-03920-0')
add(SECTIONS[0][0],'AF Ablation Versus Pacing in Selected Tachy–Brady Syndrome',
 'A 61-year-old with paroxysmal AF has symptomatic pauses confined to AF termination. Between episodes, sinus rate and exercise response are normal. LV function is preserved, and electrophysiology assessment finds the patient suitable for AF ablation. Which counseling statement is most appropriate?',
 'Ablation is a reasonable alternative strategy, but recurrent pauses may still require a pacemaker',[
 ('Normal interepisode sinus function excludes a pacing indication even if symptomatic pauses recur','Preserved sinus function between attacks does not negate symptomatic postconversion pauses. Persistent clinically important bradycardia can still warrant pacing if the chosen rhythm-control strategy fails or is unsuitable.'),
 ('A pacemaker should be described as the treatment that eliminates the AF substrate','Pacing protects against bradycardia and can facilitate medications, but does not eliminate the atrial arrhythmia substrate. Rhythm treatment and stroke prevention remain separate aspects of management.'),
 ('Ablation has established superior survival to pacing in this presentation','The available small randomized comparison did not establish a survival advantage. Better rhythm maintenance and the possibility of avoiding implantation should not be converted into proof of superior mortality outcomes.')],
 [S('Choose According to the Dominant Mechanism','**AF-triggered postconversion pauses may improve when the atrial arrhythmia is controlled.** A patient with preserved interepisode sinus function can reasonably discuss an ablation-first approach with an electrophysiology team.',
    'Persistent baseline sinus dysfunction, recurrent dangerous bradycardia, inability to control AF or a need for bradycardia-provoking treatment can favor pacing. Acute unstable events require immediate safety measures regardless of the eventual rhythm strategy.',
    'Consider age, atrial disease, comorbidity, AF burden, procedural risks and preferences. A shared decision should include the possibility of needing both ablation and pacing.'),
  S('Interpret Contemporary Randomized Evidence Cautiously','**A 2024 trial randomized 68 patients with paroxysmal AF and tachy–brady syndrome to ablation or pacemaker treatment.** At two years, the primary cardiovascular emergency-visit/hospitalization comparison was not significant.',
    'Recurrent AF was less frequent after ablation, but 4 of 35 ablation patients (11.4%) subsequently required pacemakers for recurrent syncope. The small study does not prove equivalence, a survival benefit or freedom from future pacing.',
    'These results inform selected stable patients rather than every severe sinus-node presentation. Continue rhythm surveillance after either procedure and assess anticoagulation independently of symptom improvement. Explain ablation-related risks and device-related burdens in concrete terms. Neither a reassuring interval pulse nor successful initial rhythm control removes the need to investigate recurrent syncope or reconsider pacing if symptomatic pauses persist.')],['US2018','ESC2021','TBSRCT2024'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Ambulatory ECG Selection by Symptom Frequency',
 'A stable patient has brief unexplained lightheaded episodes approximately once a week. Resting ECG and initial assessment are nondiagnostic. There is no syncope or high-risk structural disease. Which initial recording strategy is most likely to provide useful symptom–rhythm correlation with proportionate burden?',
 'A continuous external ECG patch for one to two weeks with a symptom diary',[
 ('A twenty-four-hour Holter recording','A short Holter is useful for daily or very frequent symptoms and quantifying rhythm burden. With weekly events, one day may not include an episode; a negative recording without symptoms would remain nondiagnostic.'),
 ('An implantable loop recorder','An implantable recorder can be useful for infrequent unexplained events after appropriate assessment. Weekly symptoms usually permit a reasonable external recording attempt before accepting an invasive device and its procedural burden.'),
 ('An immediate electrophysiology study','EPS can clarify selected conduction disease or arrhythmic risk when the clinical substrate supports it. In a stable patient without a high-risk ECG or structural finding, external recording more directly tests the intermittent symptom–rhythm relationship.')],
 [S('Match the Sampling Window to the Clinical Event','**The best monitor is one that can capture the relevant event with interpretable ECG data.** Symptom frequency, duration, abrupt loss of consciousness and the patient’s ability to activate a recorder influence selection.',
    'Daily symptoms often suit 24–48-hour Holter recording. Events separated by days or weeks usually require a longer external patch, loop recording or telemetry strategy; very infrequent events may justify an implantable monitor.',
    'These are practical matching principles rather than rigid calendar rules. Device availability, automatic detection, skin tolerance, adherence and the suspected arrhythmia also matter.'),
  S('Interpret a Recording as a Clinical Test','**Ask whether the typical symptom actually occurred during recording.** Sinus rhythm during a clearly recorded typical episode argues against a bradyarrhythmia mechanism for that episode.',
    'Conversely, a normal tracing over a symptom-free interval does not exclude intermittent disease. Review signal quality and manually verify device-labeled pauses because artifact or undersensing can mimic bradycardia.',
    'Provide instructions for diary entries or event marking and arrange review of clinically important alerts. An incidental asymptomatic slow rate is not automatically the cause of unrelated dizziness. Escalate evaluation if true syncope, exertional events, injury or new conduction disease changes the risk profile. The monitor should answer a defined question rather than become a reason to postpone appropriate treatment when a dangerous rhythm has already been documented.')],['US2018','ESC2021'],
 table=(['Event pattern','Common monitoring approach'],[
 ['Daily or very frequent','24–48-hour Holter'],['Days to weeks','Longer external continuous patch or loop/telemetry'],
 ['More than 30 days apart with nondiagnostic initial evaluation','Consider implantable cardiac monitoring'],
 ['Typical symptom recorded with normal rhythm','Reconsider arrhythmic attribution for that episode']]))

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Implantable Cardiac Monitoring for Infrequent Unexplained Syncope',
 'A patient has three abrupt unexplained syncopal events over ten months, separated by two to four months. History, examination, orthostatic assessment, ECG and echocardiography have not established a cause. A thirty-day external monitor was nondiagnostic and captured no event. There is no immediate pacemaker or ICD indication. Which next rhythm investigation is most appropriate?',
 'Implantable loop recorder with automatic event detection and follow-up',[
 ('Repeat twenty-four-hour Holter monitoring','A short Holter suits frequent symptoms. With episodes months apart and no event during thirty days, repeating a one-day sample has a low chance of resolving the symptom–rhythm question.'),
 ('Use a patient-activated handheld ECG at the onset of syncope','Handheld recording can document sustained palpitations when the patient can act. Abrupt loss of consciousness may prevent activation, so automatic long-term recording is more suitable for this event pattern.'),
 ('Proceed directly to pacemaker implantation','Empiric pacing is appropriate in selected separately defined high-risk situations, but no qualifying rhythm or conduction indication is supplied. Establishing the mechanism can distinguish bradycardia from tachyarrhythmia or a nonarrhythmic cause before choosing therapy.')],
 [S('Extend Monitoring When Events Are Rare','**An implantable cardiac monitor records over a much longer period than a short external study and can detect events automatically.** It is diagnostic surveillance rather than a pacing or defibrillation treatment.',
    'The US 2018 guideline considers it reasonable when suspected bradycardic symptoms are ==more than 30 days apart== and initial noninvasive evaluation is nondiagnostic.',
    'The absence of an event on a thirty-day external recording is expected to be uninformative when the typical interval is several months. Extending the observation window addresses that sampling limitation.'),
  S('Preserve Safety While Seeking a Diagnosis','**Assess risk before selecting outpatient monitoring.** Ongoing instability, documented advanced block, malignant ventricular arrhythmia or another immediate treatment indication should not wait for a future recorder event.',
    'Explain the insertion procedure, local complications, activation instructions, automatic alerts and review plan. Verify recorded pauses and tachycardias because sensing artifacts can generate misleading labels.',
    'A symptom-associated recording can guide a later device or rhythm-treatment decision, while symptoms without arrhythmia may redirect evaluation. Continue assessment of nonarrhythmic syncope causes and practical precautions appropriate to recurrence risk. Neither a normal baseline echo nor a nondiagnostic monitor proves the condition benign; the objective is to obtain meaningful event evidence without pretending that an implanted recorder itself protects the patient from a dangerous arrhythmia.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'New Left Bundle-Branch Block: Structural Assessment',
 'An asymptomatic patient has newly identified LBBB on a routine ECG. There is no acute chest pain, syncope or documented AV block, and no prior cardiac imaging is available. Which initial investigation best evaluates the associated structural substrate?',
 'Transthoracic echocardiography',[
 ('Cardiac MRI as the first imaging investigation','CMR is useful when cardiomyopathy, inflammation or infiltration remains suspected or echocardiography is insufficient. Without an initial structural study, transthoracic echo is the recommended first assessment rather than proceeding directly to advanced tissue imaging.'),
 ('Electrophysiology study','EPS may help in selected patients with syncope and conduction-system disease or uncertain block localization. It does not provide the initial structural and LV-function assessment sought in this asymptomatic presentation.'),
 ('Coronary angiography','Angiography is appropriate for a defined coronary indication, including selected acute or high-risk presentations. LBBB alone without ischemic symptoms or other supplied evidence does not establish a need for immediate invasive coronary assessment.')],
 [S('A Conduction Finding Can Signal Myocardial Disease','**New LBBB increases concern for underlying structural disease and LV systolic dysfunction.** The US 2018 guideline recommends transthoracic echocardiography when LBBB is newly identified.',
    'Assess LV and RV function, chamber dimensions and important valve disease. Compare with prior ECGs and evaluate hypertension, ischemic history, cardiomyopathy clues and family history.',
    'An echo also helps determine whether later management should address ventricular disease rather than treating the conduction pattern as an isolated electrical diagnosis.'),
  S('Let the Clinical Findings Determine Further Testing','**A newly recognized LBBB is not by itself a permanent-pacemaker indication.** Syncope, intermittent advanced block or another qualifying pacing problem changes the subsequent pathway.',
    'Use CMR or other targeted imaging when clinical suspicion persists despite an incomplete or nondiagnostic first study. Evaluate coronary disease proportionately to symptoms, risk and other findings.',
    'The stable vignette differs from acute chest pain or shock, where urgent ischemic and hemodynamic evaluation is required. A normal echo does not exclude every intermittent conduction problem; new syncope can still require monitoring or electrophysiological assessment. Conversely, a wide QRS alone does not justify CRT without the relevant ventricular, symptomatic or pacing context. Record the baseline substrate and arrange follow-up appropriate to what the assessment shows.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

R['BLOCKEDPAC']=('Primary case report illustrating blocked atrial bigeminy, 2025','https://doi.org/10.2147/IMCRJ.S555159')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Blocked Atrial Premature Beats Mimicking AV Block',
 'A rhythm strip appears to show ventricular pauses after every sinus beat. Magnification reveals a small abnormal atrial deflection deforming each preceding T wave, earlier than the next expected sinus P wave. These early deflections have no following QRS. Which diagnosis best explains the pattern?',
 'Blocked atrial bigeminy',[
 ('Two-to-one AV block of sinus impulses','In true 2:1 AV block, sinus P waves occur at the underlying atrial cycle and every other impulse fails to conduct. Here the nonconducted atrial deflection is premature and morphologically different, identifying ectopy rather than alternate block of regular sinus impulses.'),
 ('Second-degree sinoatrial exit block','SA exit block produces absent expected atrial activation, with a compatible relationship to the sinus cycle. The visible premature atrial activity hidden in the T wave supplies another mechanism for the apparent pause.'),
 ('Sinus arrest with junctional escape','Sinus arrest concerns failure of sinus impulse formation and may be followed by escape activity. It does not explain the repeated early abnormal atrial deflections that account for the apparent blocked-beat pattern here.')],
 [S('Find the Premature P Wave','**An atrial premature impulse can reach refractory AV tissue and fail to conduct.** Its P wave may be hidden in the ST segment or T wave, creating apparent sinus slowing or a ventricular pause.',
    'Compare the suspicious T wave with unaffected beats in several leads. Assess atrial timing, morphology and coupling rather than relying on the ventricular rate or an automated AV-block label.',
    'Sinus-node resetting by the premature impulse can alter the following interval. A pause after an early atrial beat should not be assumed to be a fixed-multiple SA block or intrinsic His–Purkinje failure.'),
  S('Avoid Treating a Mimic as Advanced Conduction Disease','**Confirm the mechanism before selecting permanent pacing.** A blocked premature atrial beat does not carry the same implication as nonreversible Mobitz II or high-grade block.',
    'Assess symptoms, ectopic burden, medication triggers and structural findings as appropriate. Stable incidental ectopy and symptomatic frequent blocked bigeminy require different levels of intervention.',
    'Specialist rhythm treatment, including selected ablation, may be considered when frequent ectopy produces clinically important symptoms or dysfunction; isolated case reports do not define a universal treatment regimen. Preserve a high-quality tracing and reconsider the diagnosis if the presumed block changes with careful atrial inspection. Concurrent true conduction disease remains possible, so finding ectopy does not excuse ignoring separate documented high-grade block or unexplained syncope.')],['US2018','BLOCKEDPAC'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Marked First-Degree AV Delay and AV Dyssynchrony',
 'A patient with sinus rhythm has PR 360 ms and one-to-one AV conduction. During exercise, the PR remains markedly prolonged and fatigue is reproduced despite an adequate sinus-rate rise. LV systolic function is preserved and there is no inducible ischemia. Which mechanism most plausibly links the conduction finding to symptoms?',
 'Poor atrioventricular timing impairing filling and forward output',[
 ('Chronotropic incompetence','An inadequate sinus-rate response can produce exertional symptoms, but the monitored exercise response is adequate here. The prominent abnormality is atrioventricular timing rather than failure to increase sinus rate.'),
 ('Intermittent complete AV block','Complete block requires failure of atrial impulses to conduct with independent ventricular activation. The recorded symptomatic exercise period retains one-to-one conduction, so that mechanism is not demonstrated.'),
 ('Ventricular dyssynchrony from bundle-branch block','Intraventricular activation delay can impair ventricular efficiency, particularly with a wide LBBB pattern. The supplied abnormality is a markedly long PR; a bundle-branch mechanism should not be inferred without its ECG evidence.')],
 [S('Delay Can Matter Despite One-to-One Conduction','**First-degree AV block is more accurately AV delay because every atrial impulse still conducts.** Its conventional definition is ==PR >200 ms==.',
    'Marked prolongation, often around ==300 ms or more==, can produce unfavorable atrial–ventricular timing, reduced effective filling and increased filling pressures. This resembles pacemaker syndrome despite the absence of an implanted device.',
    'The numerical boundary is not an automatic implantation trigger. Demonstrating a plausible symptom relationship, particularly during activity when delay persists, is central to clinical significance.'),
  S('Evaluate Before Selecting Pacing','**Review drugs, conduction pattern, structural disease and alternative sources of fatigue.** Exercise assessment can link symptoms with persistent long AV timing and distinguish a deficient sinus response.',
    'A narrow QRS often suggests nodal delay, but PR duration alone does not definitively localize the abnormality. A new wide QRS, syncope or higher-grade block changes risk assessment.',
    'Selected patients with symptoms attributable to marked delay may benefit from pacing assessment after reversible contributors are addressed; supporting evidence is more limited than for nonreversible high-grade block. Asymptomatic first-degree delay or symptoms unrelated to it should not be treated with a pacemaker solely to normalize the ECG. If pacing is chosen, consider how restoring AV timing interacts with ventricular pacing burden and ventricular function, rather than correcting one interval at the expense of unnecessary dyssynchrony.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Wenckebach and Mobitz II: Conducted PR Intervals',
 'A strip has a stable sinus P–P interval. Successive conducted PR intervals measure 180, 220 and 260 ms, followed by a nonconducted P wave. The next conducted PR is 180 ms. Which interpretation best fits?',
 'Second-degree AV block, Mobitz I',[
 ('Second-degree AV block, Mobitz II','Mobitz II has a nonconducted sinus impulse without the demonstrated progressive PR prolongation; conducted PR intervals before and after the blocked beat remain constant in a correctly classified pattern.'),
 ('Second-degree sinoatrial Wenckebach block','SA Wenckebach concerns atrial timing and missing atrial activation. Here P waves continue at a stable cycle and the changing PR interval demonstrates progressive delay between atrial and ventricular activation.'),
 ('Blocked atrial bigeminy','Blocked premature atrial beats are early and usually have a different atrial morphology or deform the preceding T wave. The nonconducted P here occurs on the stable sinus schedule after progressive AV delay.')],
 [S('Read Atrial Timing and AV Conduction Together','**Mobitz I describes progressive AV conduction delay culminating in a blocked atrial impulse, followed by shorter conduction time when the sequence restarts.** The supplied PR progression and reset form a classic Wenckebach sequence.',
    'Mobitz II requires stable conducted PR intervals around an intermittently blocked sinus impulse, with attention to a stable atrial rate and exclusion of mimics. A pure 2:1 strip cannot establish either type from PR progression alone.',
    'Review enough cycles to avoid mistaking subtle PR changes, concealed ectopy or vagal slowing for a different mechanism. An automated label is not a substitute for examining atrial activity.'),
  S('Separate ECG Pattern From Anatomical Risk','**Mobitz I is commonly nodal, especially with a narrow QRS, but can occasionally be infranodal.** Mobitz II generally signals His–Purkinje disease and has a different progression risk.',
    'Assess symptoms, QRS pattern, reversible causes and response to exercise or selected electrophysiological testing when localization is consequential. A wide QRS alone does not convert a Wenckebach sequence into Mobitz II.',
    'Asymptomatic nodal Wenckebach often warrants observation and cause review, whereas nonreversible Mobitz II, advanced block or complete block can require pacing regardless of symptoms. Hemodynamic compromise demands immediate stabilization before a detailed outpatient localization strategy. Preserve both descriptions in the clinical record: what the strip shows and what the total evidence suggests about the site and risk of block.')],['US2018','ESC2021'],
 table=(['Pattern','Atrial activity and conducted PR'],[
 ['Mobitz I','Progressive PR delay, blocked P, then shorter PR'],
 ['Mobitz II','Stable sinus timing; conducted PR constant around blocked impulse'],
 ['2:1 AV block','Alternate P waves blocked; no sufficient consecutive PR sequence to type'],
 ['Blocked PAC','Premature abnormal P rather than a regular sinus impulse']]))

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Two-to-One AV Block: Limits of Surface Classification',
 'A continuous ECG strip shows regular sinus P waves at 80/min with alternate P waves nonconducted. Conducted PR intervals are 190 ms and QRS duration is 132 ms. No sequence with two consecutive conducted P waves is available. Which description is most defensible?',
 'Two-to-one AV block with the anatomical level not established by this strip',[
 ('Mobitz II AV block established by the constant conducted PR intervals','In a pure 2:1 pattern, each conducted PR is separated by a blocked impulse; there is no consecutive conducted sequence to assess Wenckebach progression. Constant visible PR values alone therefore do not establish Mobitz II.'),
 ('Mobitz I AV block established by the ventricular rate of forty','A ventricular rate of forty follows from alternate conduction at an atrial rate of eighty. It does not demonstrate progressive AV delay or distinguish nodal from infranodal block.'),
 ('Infranodal block established by the wide QRS','A wide QRS increases concern for His–Purkinje disease but is not definitive localization. Bundle-branch disease can coexist with nodal block; additional clinical and physiological evidence may be needed.')],
 [S('Describe What the Recording Actually Demonstrates','**Two-to-one conduction is a pattern, not proof of either Mobitz subtype.** Alternate atrial impulses are blocked, preventing observation of the consecutive PR behavior needed for the usual type I/type II distinction.',
    'Verify regular sinus activity and exclude premature blocked atrial beats, artifact and concealed ectopy. Measure QRS and conducted PR, but do not use either alone as an anatomical verdict.',
    'Review longer telemetry for another conduction ratio or a diagnostic sequence. A transition can provide information unavailable from the short pure 2:1 segment.'),
  S('Localize When It Changes Risk and Treatment','**The site of block matters because infranodal disease can progress with an unreliable escape rhythm.** Symptoms, structural disease and associated conduction abnormalities determine urgency.',
    'Selected stable patients can undergo supervised exercise testing; improved conduction supports a nodal site, while deterioration raises concern for infranodal disease. EPS can resolve consequential uncertainty when surface assessment remains insufficient.',
    'Do not use diagnostic uncertainty as a reason to delay stabilization of a compromised patient. Conversely, do not implant solely from an unsupported Mobitz II label when the stable clinical setting calls for clarification. Nonreversible infranodal 2:1 block and asymptomatic convincingly nodal 2:1 block have different management implications. Document the measured pattern and the evidence for localization separately so later decisions do not inherit an unproven interpretation.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Exercise Response and AV Block Localization',
 'During supervised exercise evaluation of stable 2:1 AV block of uncertain level, the atrial rate increases and conduction deteriorates to 3:1 with reproduction of lightheadedness. The test is stopped and the patient is assessed promptly. Which interpretation best guides further management?',
 'The response raises concern for infranodal conduction disease requiring urgent specialist pacing assessment',[
 ('The response supports physiological vagal nodal block','Vagally mediated nodal block commonly improves as vagal tone withdraws with exercise. Deterioration despite increasing sympathetic drive is concerning for a different mechanism, particularly when symptoms occur.'),
 ('The response establishes sinus chronotropic incompetence','The sinus rate increases, while fewer atrial impulses reach the ventricles. The limitation is worsening AV conduction rather than inadequate sinus acceleration.'),
 ('The response establishes exercise-induced myocardial ischemia as the cause','Ischemia can occasionally contribute to exercise-related conduction abnormalities and should be considered when supported by the presentation. Worsening block alone does not establish coronary ischemia rather than intrinsic infranodal disease.')],
 [S('Use Physiology to Refine the Anatomical Hypothesis','**Exercise generally improves AV nodal conduction but may expose diseased His–Purkinje tissue as atrial impulses arrive faster.** Deterioration of conduction therefore supports concern for an infranodal site.',
    'This is a clinically useful inference rather than direct histological proof. Integrate the QRS pattern, symptoms, structural findings, drug exposure and the actual exercise recording.',
    'Improvement of an asymptomatic narrow-QRS 2:1 pattern, especially with accompanying Wenckebach, supports a supra-Hisian explanation. That reassuring scenario differs from symptomatic progression to a higher conduction ratio.'),
  S('Prioritize Safety and Resolve Remaining Uncertainty','**Stop testing for clinically important symptomatic deterioration and arrange monitored assessment.** Exercise is a diagnostic tool for selected stable patients, not a stress challenge to continue through dangerous block.',
    'EPS can define nodal, intra-Hisian or infra-Hisian disease when localization remains uncertain and will change management. Evaluate reversible causes and selected ischemic or inflammatory explanations according to the full presentation.',
    'Confirmed nonreversible advanced infranodal disease supports permanent pacing; unstable patients may need temporary support while a definitive plan is made. Do not reassure solely because the resting strip had a modest ventricular rate or the patient was initially asymptomatic. Equally, do not assume that every exercise-associated symptom is conduction-related without a recorded association. The reproduced symptom with documented worsening block supplies the consequential link here.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'High-Grade AV Block With Residual Conduction',
 'A prolonged ECG recording shows sinus P waves at 90/min. Two consecutive P waves repeatedly fail to conduct, followed by a P wave conducting with PR 180 ms. Each ventricular complex is linked to that third P wave; no independent escape rhythm is present. Which description best fits?',
 'High-grade AV block with three-to-one conduction',[
 ('Complete AV block with an escape rhythm at thirty per minute','Complete block has no effective atrioventricular conduction. Here each ventricular response is demonstrably conducted from a sinus P wave with a reproducible PR, so residual conduction remains.'),
 ('Two-to-one AV block','A 2:1 pattern blocks alternate P waves and conducts every second impulse. The supplied record blocks two successive impulses and conducts the third, establishing a 3:1 rather than 2:1 relationship.'),
 ('Second-degree sinoatrial exit block','SA exit block produces failure of expected atrial activation. Sinus P waves continue regularly here; the failure occurs between atrial and ventricular activation rather than before the visible P wave.')],
 [S('Count Atrial Impulses Before Naming the Pattern','**High-grade or advanced AV block includes two or more consecutive nonconducted sinus P waves with some remaining AV conduction.** The supplied 3:1 pattern meets that definition.',
    'Confirm that the atrial rate is physiological and that the nonconducted deflections are sinus impulses rather than premature ectopy. Atrial tachycardia or flutter with a conduction ratio requires a different interpretation.',
    'A slow ventricular rate alone cannot distinguish conducted responses from escape activity. Follow P-to-QRS timing over enough cycles to establish whether a causal conduction relationship exists.'),
  S('Distinguish Severity From Exact Localization','**High-grade block is a concerning conduction finding even when every third impulse still reaches the ventricles.** The anatomical level and reversibility require assessment; the ratio alone does not provide them.',
    'Assess perfusion, syncope, QRS pattern, drugs, electrolytes, ischemia and relevant inflammatory or infectious causes. Unstable bradycardia requires monitored resuscitation and pacing support as appropriate.',
    'Nonreversible acquired high-grade AV block supports permanent pacing even without reported symptoms under the named US guideline. Temporary improvement in one recording does not establish durable recovery. Conversely, complete AV dissociation should not be assigned when clear conducted beats remain. Communicate both the atrial/ventricular rates and observed conduction pattern so urgency is understood without overstating the degree or anatomical certainty.')],['US2018','ESC2021'],
 table=(['Pattern','Defining relationship'],[
 ['2:1 AV block','Every second sinus P conducts'],['High-grade AV block','At least two consecutive P waves blocked; some conduction remains'],
 ['Complete AV block','No demonstrated AV conduction; subsidiary ventricular activation'],
 ['SA exit block','Expected atrial activation is absent rather than a visible P failing to conduct']]))

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'AV Dissociation Without Persistent Complete Heart Block',
 'A stable patient has sinus P waves at 58/min and a narrow-QRS junctional rhythm at 62/min, with varying P–QRS relationships. During observation, the sinus rate increases to 78/min and one-to-one conduction resumes with PR 170 ms. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'Interference AV dissociation with demonstrable preserved AV conduction',[
 ('Persistent complete AV block','The dissociated segment can resemble complete block, but subsequent ordinary one-to-one sinus conduction establishes that persistent complete interruption is not present. Intermittent disease would require separate evidence.'),
 ('Two-to-one AV block','A 2:1 pattern requires alternate sinus impulses conducting to the ventricles. The initial segment instead has a slightly faster independent junctional rhythm and no fixed alternate conduction relationship.'),
 ('Sinus arrest with junctional escape','Sinus arrest would lack continuing sinus atrial depolarizations during the pause. Regular sinus P waves remain visible here; their rate is temporarily slower than the competing junctional rhythm.')],
 [S('AV Dissociation Describes Independence, Not One Etiology','**Atria and ventricles may be activated independently when a subsidiary rhythm competes with the sinus node, even if the AV conduction system can conduct.** This is interference dissociation rather than proof of complete block.',
    'A slowing sinus rhythm or accelerated junctional/ventricular focus can create this pattern. Similar atrial and ventricular rates can produce shifting apparent PR relationships.',
    'The return of one-to-one sinus conduction as the atrial rate increases demonstrates preserved conduction at that time. Do not describe that finding as exclusion of every possible intermittent conduction disorder.'),
  S('Identify What Is Driving the Competing Rhythms','**Compare atrial and ventricular rates, QRS morphology, timing and any capture of ventricular activation by sinus impulses.** Examine a sufficiently long strip rather than treating one dissociated snapshot as a complete diagnosis.',
    'Review drugs, autonomic state, ischemia and other causes of subsidiary-rhythm acceleration or sinus slowing. Wide-complex tachycardia with AV dissociation creates a different urgent diagnostic context from this stable narrow-QRS rhythm.',
    'A permanent pacemaker should not be chosen merely because the words AV dissociation appear on a tracing report. Treat an established cause or independently documented pacing indication. New syncope, progressive conduction abnormalities or recurrent unexplained pauses can still justify monitoring and further assessment. Document the evidence for actual block separately from the observation that atrial and ventricular activation were temporarily independent.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Permanent Pacing for Nonreversible Mobitz II AV Block',
 'An adult has repeatedly documented acquired Mobitz II AV block, verified on recordings with consecutive conducted beats and stable sinus timing. Medication, electrolyte, ischemic and other reversible evaluations are unrevealing. The patient reports no syncope or exercise limitation and has a reasonable long-term prognosis. Which approach is appropriate?',
 'Recommend permanent pacing after assessment of the appropriate device system',[
 ('Use ambulatory surveillance until syncope establishes symptom correlation','Symptom correlation is central to many sinus-node and nodal-delay decisions, but nonreversible acquired Mobitz II has a prognostic pacing indication. Waiting for syncope can expose the patient to progression and unreliable escape rhythms.'),
 ('Perform exercise testing to determine whether symptoms can be provoked before recommending pacing','Exercise can help localize uncertain 2:1 block or assess an unresolved symptom relationship. The stem establishes true Mobitz II without a reversible cause, so provoking symptoms is not a prerequisite to pacing.'),
 ('Prescribe an oral chronotropic agent with periodic ECG review','Chronotropic treatment has selected temporary or specialist roles but does not reliably protect against progression of diseased His–Purkinje conduction. It is not a substitute for the established permanent-pacing indication in this setting.')],
 [S('A Prognostic Indication Differs From Symptom Relief Alone','**The US 2018 guideline recommends permanent pacing for acquired Mobitz II, high-grade or third-degree AV block not attributable to reversible or physiological causes, regardless of symptoms.** The absence of reported events does not make progression harmless.',
    'Confirm the ECG classification and exclude mimics such as blocked ectopy or an untyped pure 2:1 strip. A confidently established diagnosis is necessary before applying the recommendation.',
    'Review reversible drug effects, metabolic abnormalities, acute ischemia and relevant inflammatory or infectious causes; some require temporary support while the cause is treated.'),
  S('Select the Device From the Whole Clinical Substrate','**A pacing indication does not by itself determine chamber configuration or whether defibrillation is needed.** Assess atrial rhythm, LV function, expected pacing burden and disease-specific ventricular arrhythmia risk.',
    'Consider physiological ventricular activation when the anticipated pacing burden and ventricular function make it appropriate. A narrow escape complex or current hemodynamic stability does not remove the need to address established nonreversible advanced conduction disease.',
    'Discuss procedural risks, follow-up, generator replacement and patient goals. If the patient declines implantation, document informed preferences and a realistic risk plan rather than describing monitoring as an equivalent protective treatment. New hypotension, syncope or prolonged ventricular pauses require urgent monitored stabilization while definitive treatment is arranged.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Asymptomatic Nodal Wenckebach',
 'A healthy adult has incidental narrow-QRS Wenckebach at rest. There is no syncope, presyncope or exercise limitation. During supervised exercise, conduction becomes one-to-one with appropriate heart-rate acceleration; echocardiography is normal and no rate-slowing drug is taken. Which approach is most appropriate?',
 'Clinical observation with reassessment if symptoms or conduction findings change',[
 ('Permanent dual-chamber pacemaker implantation','Pacing can be appropriate for attributable symptoms or selected infranodal disease. This asymptomatic, exercise-improving narrow-QRS pattern supports nodal physiology rather than a supplied prognostic pacing indication.'),
 ('Electrophysiology study before allowing ordinary activity','EPS can resolve consequential uncertainty, particularly with syncope or suspected infranodal disease. The reassuring clinical and exercise pattern does not establish a routine need for an invasive localization procedure here.'),
 ('Chronic oral chronotropic therapy','Chronotropic treatment is not needed to normalize an incidental asymptomatic nodal pattern. It introduces adverse effects without a demonstrated symptomatic or hemodynamic problem requiring rate support.')],
 [S('Integrate Pattern, Site and Symptoms','**Wenckebach commonly reflects AV nodal decremental conduction.** Narrow QRS, lack of structural disease and improvement during exercise support a lower-risk nodal mechanism in this vignette.',
    'The US 2018 guideline advises against permanent pacing for asymptomatic first-degree delay or Mobitz I/2:1 block believed to be nodal. The rule should not be generalized to proven infranodal Wenckebach.',
    'Some patients have symptoms from significant bradycardia or impaired AV timing; others have conduction disease beyond the node. Those features require a different assessment rather than a label-based assumption of benignity.'),
  S('Observe With a Defined Reassessment Plan','**Review potential reversible contributors and explain which new events warrant evaluation.** Syncope, exertional lightheadedness, worsening exercise conduction or new bundle-branch disease can change the risk profile.',
    'No universal monitoring interval applies to every incidental finding. Tailor clinical review and repeat ECG or ambulatory recording to symptoms, age, comorbidity and evolving evidence.',
    'If symptoms emerge, document their rhythm relationship before attributing them to the incidental Wenckebach. A normal rhythm during a typical symptom can redirect the differential, while documented worsening block supports escalation. Observation means proportionate follow-up of a reassuring current phenotype, not a promise that conduction cannot change or a reason to dismiss later clinically important events.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Vagally Mediated AV Block',
 'A young adult has an incidental nocturnal rhythm event without symptoms. Before transient AV block, sinus P–P intervals lengthen and conducted PR intervals prolong; both normalize as the sinus rate recovers. Daytime ECG, exercise conduction and echocardiography are normal. Which mechanism is most likely?',
 'Transient vagally mediated AV nodal block',[
 ('Intrinsic paroxysmal His–Purkinje block','Intrinsic paroxysmal block can cause abrupt dangerous ventricular pauses, often in a substrate of conduction disease. The coordinated sinus slowing and nodal delay around this transient event support an autonomic mechanism instead.'),
 ('Blocked atrial premature activity','Blocked ectopy requires premature atrial activation, often with changed morphology or a P wave hidden in the preceding T wave. Here atrial intervals lengthen rather than an early ectopic impulse initiating the pause.'),
 ('Sinus arrest','Sinus arrest produces absent atrial activation during the pause. Visible P waves fail to conduct in the described event, establishing an AV component in addition to the accompanying sinus slowing.')],
 [S('Look for Coordinated Sinus and AV Nodal Effects','**Increased vagal activity can slow sinus discharge and AV nodal conduction together.** Sinus slowing around the onset of AV block is a useful clue when interpreted with the whole recording.',
    'The event can appear high-grade or even transiently complete, yet its physiological cause differs from persistent intrinsic conduction-system disease. Assess timing, triggers, QRS pattern, medications and structural findings.',
    'Sleep-associated events also warrant a sleep-disordered-breathing history when clinically indicated. Do not assign the mechanism merely because an event occurred at night; the accompanying atrial and AV behavior matters.'),
  S('Separate Incidental Events From Symptomatic Reflex Syncope','**Asymptomatic convincingly vagally mediated AV block does not warrant permanent pacing under the US 2018 framework.** This differs from nonreversible intrinsic advanced block.',
    'Recurrent symptomatic reflex syncope requires a separate assessment of documented asystole, hypotensive susceptibility, age, injury risk and treatment options. A pacemaker may not correct the vasodepressor component even when bradycardia contributes.',
    'The supplied normal daytime and exercise assessment supports conservative clinical follow-up. New unexplained syncope or worsening baseline conduction should reopen the diagnosis. Avoid treating one autonomic clue as absolute exclusion of coexisting disease, but also avoid converting an incidental physiological pattern into a prognostic pacing indication. Accurate symptom history and a verified tracing prevent both errors.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Syncope With Bifascicular Block and a Prolonged HV Interval',
 'A patient has recurrent unexplained syncope, right bundle-branch block with left anterior fascicular block and preserved LV function. Electrophysiology study shows a baseline HV interval of 78 ms. No reversible cause or competing established diagnosis is found. Under ESC 2021 guidance, which strategy is appropriate?',
 'Recommend permanent pacemaker implantation',[
 ('Insert a loop recorder and defer pacing until spontaneous complete block is captured','An implantable recorder can be valuable after a nondiagnostic evaluation or negative EPS. The supplied prolonged HV meets the guideline’s positive electrophysiological criterion in syncope with bifascicular block, so another spontaneous event is not required.'),
 ('Repeat EPS only if the baseline HV reaches one hundred milliseconds','One hundred milliseconds is not the baseline threshold in the named syncope framework. Waiting for that value would miss patients meeting the baseline 70-ms criterion; pharmacological-challenge interpretation is a separate context.'),
 ('Implant an ICD on the basis of the prolonged HV interval','An ICD is selected for a separate ventricular arrhythmia or sudden-death indication. Prolonged His–ventricular conduction identifies a pacing problem, not by itself a defibrillation indication in this preserved-LV presentation.')],
 [S('Use the Correct Electrophysiological Boundary','**ESC 2021 indicates pacing in unexplained syncope with bifascicular block when baseline HV is ==≥70 ms== or other qualifying intra-/infra-Hisian block is demonstrated.** The measured 78 ms meets that criterion.',
    'HV measures conduction from His activation to ventricular activation. Prolongation indicates disease distal to the AV node and helps identify risk of intermittent advanced block.',
    'Induced second- or third-degree intra-/infra-Hisian block during atrial pacing or an abnormal pharmacological challenge can also establish a positive study. Do not interchange baseline and provoked thresholds.'),
  S('Understand What a Negative Study Would Mean','**A negative EPS does not exclude intermittent arrhythmic syncope.** Continued monitoring, often with an implantable recorder, may be appropriate when uncertainty remains after a negative study.',
    'Selected older patients with recurrent unpredictable traumatic syncope may be considered for empirical pacing after individualized assessment. This is different from the positive-study indication already present here.',
    'Assess alternative syncope mechanisms as well because hypotension and reflex susceptibility can coexist with conduction disease. Pacing can address intermittent block without guaranteeing elimination of every faint. Before implantation, review atrial rhythm, anticipated pacing burden, ventricular function and any independent ICD indication. Explain procedural risks and follow-up while avoiding a delay that requires another injury before acting on the documented high-risk substrate.')],['ESC2021','US2018'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Alternating Bundle-Branch Block',
 'Serial ECGs from an adult show a convincing LBBB pattern on one recording and RBBB on another, both at similar sinus rates. Expert review excludes lead-placement error and a simple rate-dependent pattern. No reversible cause is found. There is no syncope. Under ESC 2021, which approach is appropriate?',
 'Recommend permanent pacing because alternating bundle-branch block indicates advanced conduction disease',[
 ('Continue routine observation until syncope occurs','Observation is often appropriate for an isolated asymptomatic bundle-branch pattern. True alternating BBB is a specific exception because it indicates disease involving the conduction system on both sides and risk of advanced AV block.'),
 ('Use an implantable recorder before considering pacing','A recorder helps establish a mechanism in unresolved intermittent symptoms. The documented alternating BBB already supplies a guideline pacing indication; another captured clinical event is not required.'),
 ('Require an HV interval of at least seventy milliseconds before recommending pacing','HV testing is important in the separate unexplained-syncope/bifascicular-block pathway. It is not an additional prerequisite to the indication supplied by convincingly established alternating BBB.')],
 [S('Recognize Bilateral Conduction-System Involvement','**Alternating BBB means different recordings demonstrate block in different bundle systems.** A genuine alternation between RBBB and LBBB indicates advanced His–Purkinje disease rather than a single stable branch abnormality.',
    'Confirm morphology, recording quality, lead placement and the clinical context. Changing QRS appearance caused by ventricular ectopy, pacing or technical error should not be mislabeled as alternating native conduction disease.',
    'ESC 2021 indicates permanent pacing with or without symptoms. The absence of syncope does not reduce this pattern to the usual observation rule for isolated asymptomatic BBB.'),
  S('Apply the Appropriate Pacing Pathway','**Assess reversible causes and select the device according to atrial rhythm, ventricular function and anticipated pacing needs.** Defibrillation capability requires its own risk indication.',
    'Explain progression risk and the rationale for treatment before an unpredictable episode of complete block. Acute compromise requires immediate stabilization while a durable plan is arranged.',
    'Avoid transferring the HV threshold from a different syncope algorithm as a mandatory extra test when the alternating pattern is already established. Conversely, two ambiguous ECG labels without expert confirmation are not sufficient evidence for an irreversible intervention. Preserve the original tracings and document the morphology and context supporting the diagnosis, then discuss procedural risks, follow-up and patient preferences in the pacing decision.')],['ESC2021','US2018'])

R['LYME2020']=('AAN/ACR/IDSA Lyme disease guideline, 2020','https://www.idsociety.org/practice-guideline/lyme-disease/')
R['LYMECDC']=('CDC clinical care and antibiotic regimens for Lyme carditis','https://www.cdc.gov/lyme/hcp/clinical-care/lyme-carditis.html')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Lyme Carditis: Reversible High-Grade AV Block',
 'A young adult develops presyncope and fluctuating high-grade AV block after travel to a Lyme-endemic region, a compatible expanding skin lesion and subsequent systemic symptoms. The patient is admitted with monitoring; testing is being arranged and Lyme carditis is strongly suspected. Which initial strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Begin IV ceftriaxone and provide temporary pacing if clinically required while assessing recovery',[
 ('Implant a permanent pacemaker immediately and treat the infection afterward','Permanent pacing is appropriate for established nonreversible advanced block. Lyme-associated conduction disease often recovers, so initial antibiotics and temporary support when needed avoid premature commitment to a permanent system.'),
 ('Use outpatient oral doxycycline with early ECG review','Oral therapy can be appropriate for mild carditis without high-risk conduction findings. Symptomatic high-grade block requires monitored inpatient management and initial IV treatment rather than outpatient observation.'),
 ('Await serological confirmation before starting antibiotics','Testing helps establish the diagnosis, but strongly suspected clinically significant carditis should be treated promptly. Waiting for results can leave a rapidly fluctuating conduction problem without cause-directed therapy.')],
 [S('Treat a Potentially Reversible Cause Without Ignoring Immediate Risk','**Lyme carditis can progress rapidly through different degrees of AV block.** Exposure and compatible clinical findings should prompt targeted testing, monitored assessment and timely treatment.',
    'High-grade block, significant arrhythmia, myopericarditis or marked PR prolongation warrants admission. A ==PR around 300 ms or longer== is an important risk boundary in published guidance, not a reassurance threshold in a patient already showing advanced block.',
    'Temporary pacing is preferred when symptomatic bradycardia cannot be managed medically and recovery is expected. Circulatory instability still requires immediate support; reversibility is not permission to leave a dangerous rate untreated.'),
  S('Use an Appropriate Antibiotic Course','**CDC adult severe-carditis treatment uses ceftriaxone ==2 g IV once daily==.** IDSA guidance supports initial IV treatment in hospitalized patients, followed by oral treatment after clinical improvement to complete ==14–21 days total==.',
    'An oral step-down option such as doxycycline 100 mg twice daily can be selected when appropriate, considering individual contraindications and other Lyme manifestations. The total course includes the IV days rather than adding a full new course automatically.',
    'Follow conduction, symptoms and any myocardial involvement during recovery. Persistent block despite adequate treatment requires reassessment of the diagnosis, substrate and permanent-device need rather than assuming recovery is inevitable. Epidemiological plausibility matters: do not label unexplained AV block Lyme disease solely from an isolated test without compatible exposure and illness.')],['LYME2020','LYMECDC','US2018'])

R['SARCOID2024']=('AHA cardiac sarcoidosis scientific statement, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001240')
R['SARCOIDESC2024']=('ESC clinical consensus on cardiac sarcoidosis, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehae356')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Unexplained AV Block and Cardiac Sarcoidosis',
 'A 46-year-old has unexplained high-grade AV block requiring monitored support. Coronary disease, drug effects and metabolic causes have been excluded. Echo shows basal septal thinning despite only mildly impaired global LV function. Which further assessment most directly addresses an important underlying diagnosis before definitive device selection?',
 'Evaluate cardiac sarcoidosis with CMR and appropriately prepared FDG-PET as indicated',[
 ('Use serum ACE concentration as the principal confirmatory investigation','Serum ACE can contribute to a systemic assessment but lacks the sensitivity and specificity to confirm or exclude cardiac sarcoidosis. It does not replace myocardial imaging in this high-risk cardiac presentation.'),
 ('Perform an unguided endomyocardial biopsy and exclude sarcoidosis if it is negative','Histology can establish the diagnosis, but patchy involvement limits an unguided cardiac biopsy’s sensitivity. A negative sample does not exclude disease; imaging and an accessible extracardiac biopsy target can guide a more informative strategy.'),
 ('Repeat echocardiography after pacemaker implantation before investigating etiology','Serial echo can follow ventricular function, but it does not adequately characterize inflammatory activity or scar. Delaying etiological assessment can also miss information relevant to choosing a defibrillator-capable rather than pacing-only device.')],
 [S('Recognize a Disease-Specific Conduction Presentation','**Unexplained advanced AV block in an adult younger than 60 years should prompt consideration of cardiac sarcoidosis.** Basal septal abnormalities, ventricular arrhythmias or extracardiac clues strengthen suspicion.',
    'CMR assesses scar and other myocardial features; FDG-PET evaluates inflammatory activity after appropriate preparation to suppress physiological myocardial uptake. Neither an isolated imaging sign nor a blood marker should be treated as an infallible diagnosis.',
    'Seek extracardiac involvement and a suitable tissue target when relevant. Patchy disease can make an unguided endomyocardial biopsy negative despite cardiac involvement.'),
  S('Connect Etiology With Device and Disease Treatment','**Sarcoidosis-associated AV block can coexist with clinically important ventricular arrhythmia risk despite relatively preserved EF.** Assess ICD capability when permanent pacing is required rather than assuming a conventional pacemaker is sufficient.',
    'Immunosuppressive treatment of active disease is a multidisciplinary decision informed by diagnostic probability, activity and risk. Potential conduction improvement does not automatically remove the need for protection against recurrent block or ventricular arrhythmia.',
    'Maintain immediate circulatory safety while investigation proceeds; imaging must not postpone necessary temporary support. Discuss the distinction between treating inflammation, preventing bradycardia and protecting against sudden arrhythmic death. Follow ventricular function, rhythm and disease activity after treatment, and reconsider alternative diagnoses such as other inflammatory or genetic cardiomyopathies when findings are discordant.')],['SARCOID2024','SARCOIDESC2024','US2018'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Transient AV Block During Inferior Myocardial Infarction',
 'During an inferior STEMI, a patient develops complete AV block with a narrow-QRS escape rhythm. Reperfusion is performed and temporary support is provided as needed. Over the following days, stable one-to-one conduction returns, with no recurrent block during appropriate observation. There is no other device indication. Which long-term pacing decision is most appropriate?',
 'Do not implant a permanent pacemaker solely for the resolved transient infarct-related block',[
 ('Implant a permanent pacemaker because complete block occurred during the acute event','The initial severity determines immediate support needs but does not by itself prove a permanent substrate. Transient infarct-related block that resolves after treatment does not supply the same long-term indication as persistent nonreversible block.'),
 ('Use a permanent pacemaker whenever temporary transvenous pacing was required','Temporary pacing treats an acute safety problem while reversibility is assessed. Its use is not itself proof that permanent pacing remains necessary after stable conduction recovery.'),
 ('Implant an ICD instead of a pacemaker because the event occurred during STEMI','Defibrillation indications depend on ventricular arrhythmias, ventricular function, timing and other criteria. A resolved transient AV block does not independently establish an ICD indication.')],
 [S('Reperfusion and Recovery Change the Pacing Assessment','**Inferior-infarct AV block may reflect transient nodal ischemia or vagal effects.** A narrow escape rhythm and subsequent recovery support this possibility but do not eliminate the need for acute monitoring.',
    'Treat the coronary cause promptly and use temporary pacing for refractory symptomatic or hemodynamically important bradycardia when appropriate. Acute support and a permanent-device decision are separate steps.',
    'The US 2018 guideline advises against permanent pacing for transient infarct-related AV block that resolves. An adequate observation period and the stability of recovery matter.'),
  S('Identify Findings That Would Change the Decision','**Persistent Mobitz II, high-grade, alternating bundle-branch or persistent/infranodal complete block after an appropriate waiting period can warrant permanent pacing.** Infarct territory alone is not a complete decision rule.',
    'Anterior infarction with new intraventricular disease can indicate extensive myocardial injury and a different conduction risk. Assess ventricular function and any separate sudden-death indication before selecting a system.',
    'Do not import a generic ICD waiting period as the pacing waiting rule, and do not delay necessary support in an unstable patient while awaiting recovery. Conversely, an early dramatic tracing should not commit a recovered patient to an unnecessary lifelong device. Document the mechanism, treatment, duration of observed recovery and follow-up plan; recurrent syncope or renewed conduction abnormalities require prompt reassessment.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

R['AHAALS2025']=('AHA adult advanced life support guideline, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001376')
R['AHABRADY2025']=('AHA adult bradycardia with a pulse algorithm, 2025','https://cpr.heart.org/en/resuscitation-science/cpr-and-ecc-guidelines/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Algorithms/Algorithm-ACLS-Bradycardia-250514.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Bradycardia With Acute Hemodynamic Compromise',
 'A patient with a pulse has a new sinus rate of 36/min, BP 76/44 mmHg, cool extremities and confusion. The deterioration coincides with the slow rhythm. Which initial approach is most appropriate?',
 'Activate monitored resuscitation, support airway and circulation, treat causes and begin the symptomatic-bradycardia pathway',[
 ('Give atropine and reassess after the full course before preparing additional support','Atropine is reasonable for symptomatic sinus bradycardia, but profound compromise requires concurrent monitoring, resuscitation and preparation for escalation. An isolated drug-first sequence can delay support when the initial response is inadequate.'),
 ('Give fluid replacement as the primary treatment before considering rate support','Fluid can help a demonstrated volume deficit, but hypotension does not itself establish hypovolemia. The severe coincident bradycardia requires immediate rate/perfusion assessment and support while causes are evaluated.'),
 ('Obtain the ECG and electrolyte results before starting rate-directed treatment','ECG and laboratory testing help identify the mechanism and reversible causes. They should be obtained alongside stabilization rather than used as prerequisites to treatment of documented bradycardic shock.')],
 [S('Treat the Physiological Consequence, Not a Rate Alone','**Acute bradycardia is clinically important when it causes or contributes to compromised perfusion.** Hypotension, acute altered mental status, shock, ischemic discomfort or acute HF identify a need for urgent assessment and treatment.',
    'The AHA algorithm describes bradyarrhythmia as typically below 50/min, but a numerical pulse boundary does not by itself define instability. Determine whether the rhythm explains deterioration or accompanies another dominant problem.',
    'Confirm a pulse and rhythm; a pulseless patient requires the cardiac-arrest pathway rather than the bradycardia-with-a-pulse algorithm.'),
  S('Stabilize and Search for Reversible Causes Together','**Obtain monitoring, access and a diagnostic ECG without delaying essential support.** Maintain the airway, assist ventilation when needed and correct hypoxemia.',
    'Assess ischemia, hypoxia, hyperkalemia, drugs and other reversible causes. Atropine is reasonable for appropriate symptomatic bradycardia; ineffective treatment or a high-risk block pattern can require pacing and/or a chronotropic infusion.',
    'Reassess pulse, pressure, mentation and organ perfusion after each intervention. A faster monitor rate without improved perfusion is not sufficient evidence of successful treatment. Prepare transcutaneous support and expert consultation early when deterioration is substantial, and consider temporary transvenous pacing if reliable sustained support is needed. The eventual permanent-device decision follows the rhythm diagnosis, reversibility and clinical substrate rather than the first low pulse alone.')],['AHAALS2025','AHABRADY2025','US2018'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Atropine in the AHA 2025 Bradycardia Algorithm',
 'An adult has acute symptomatic sinus bradycardia with a pulse and hemodynamic compromise. Atropine is selected under the AHA 2025 algorithm while monitoring and rescue pacing are prepared. Which IV regimen matches that algorithm?',
 '1 mg bolus, repeated every 3–5 minutes if needed, to 3 mg total',[
 ('0.5 mg bolus, repeated every 3–5 minutes if needed, to 3 mg total','This resembles an older AHA regimen and a regimen retained in some other resuscitation frameworks. The question specifically names the AHA 2025 algorithm, which starts with 1 mg IV.'),
 ('1 mg bolus, repeated every 3–5 minutes if needed, to 6 mg total','The initial dose and interval match, but 6 mg is not the maximum in the named bradycardia algorithm. Refractory compromise requires another support strategy rather than extending the atropine ceiling.'),
 ('0.5 mg bolus, repeated every 1–2 minutes if needed, to 3 mg total','This combines a familiar smaller bolus with a shorter repeat interval. Neither matches the named AHA 2025 adult regimen; a shared cumulative ceiling does not make the initial dose and timing equivalent.')],
 [S('Use the Named Current Regimen','**The AHA 2025 adult algorithm specifies atropine ==1 mg IV==, repeated every ==3–5 minutes== to a maximum ==3 mg total==.** Document dose and response while continuing the resuscitation assessment.',
    'Atropine antagonizes muscarinic effects and can increase sinus rate and AV nodal conduction. It is less reliable for advanced infranodal disease than for vagally mediated sinus or nodal slowing.',
    'Guideline year and organization matter because a 0.5-mg initial dose appears in older AHA material and other contemporary frameworks. An undated universal dosing question can therefore become ambiguous.'),
  S('Escalate According to Perfusion and Block Pattern','**Do not wait to exhaust repeated atropine doses when the patient needs immediate pacing support.** Persistent hypotension, altered mentation or worsening shock requires prompt reassessment and escalation.',
    'AHA permits transcutaneous pacing and/or dopamine or epinephrine infusion when atropine is ineffective, with expert consultation and temporary transvenous pacing as required.',
    'Monitor rhythm and ischemic symptoms because excessive rate acceleration can increase myocardial oxygen demand. Treat hyperkalemia, hypoxia, drug toxicity and ischemia directly rather than relying on atropine alone. A transient pulse response does not prove durable recovery of diseased conduction tissue or settle permanent-pacing eligibility. This regimen applies to an adult with a pulse; it should not be transferred to pulseless cardiac arrest or organophosphate poisoning.')],['AHABRADY2025','AHAALS2025'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Emergency Pacing for High-Grade Infranodal Block',
 'A patient with known bifascicular disease develops high-grade AV block with a broad escape rhythm at 24/min, BP 64/38 mmHg and declining consciousness. Atropine 1 mg IV has produced no improvement. Pacing equipment is available. Which next step best addresses the immediate risk?',
 'Begin transcutaneous pacing promptly while arranging reliable temporary support and treating reversible causes',[
 ('Complete the remaining atropine doses before starting transcutaneous pacing','Additional atropine can help some sinus or nodal bradycardias, but severe ongoing shock with suspected distal conduction disease makes waiting for the full cumulative dose unsafe. Rescue pacing should proceed without that delay.'),
 ('Start a dopamine infusion and defer pacing preparation until its maximal dose is reached','Dopamine is an accepted monitored bridge and can be started alongside other support. Reaching its maximum dose is not a prerequisite for pacing preparation in profound shock with advanced conduction disease.'),
 ('Transfer directly for permanent pacemaker implantation before providing temporary rate support','Permanent pacing may be required after evaluation, but transfer and implantation take time. Immediate circulatory support must bridge that process while reversible causes and the appropriate definitive device are assessed.')],
 [S('Distal Conduction Disease and Atropine Response','**High-grade AV block with a slow broad escape suggests a dangerous distal conduction substrate.** Surface QRS width supports localization but does not establish the precise site by itself.',
    'Atropine predominantly removes vagal restraint on the sinus and AV nodes. It may fail to improve ventricular rate when impulses cannot traverse diseased His–Purkinje tissue; acceleration of atrial input does not ensure effective ventricular activation.',
    'The immediate decision follows perfusion and response. Persistent profound hypotension and deteriorating consciousness after an initial trial justify prompt rescue support rather than waiting to complete a numerical drug sequence.'),
  S('Bridge to Reliable Support','**Apply transcutaneous pacing with continuous assessment of capture and perfusion.** A pacing spike or displayed rate alone does not establish an effective circulation.',
    'An epinephrine or dopamine infusion can provide additional temporary support while expertise and transvenous pacing are arranged. The available evidence does not establish one universally superior bridge for every setting.',
    'Provide carefully titrated analgesia or sedation when feasible without postponing life-saving pacing or worsening hemodynamics. Correct ischemia, hypoxia, electrolyte disturbance and drug effects in parallel. Transvenous pacing is reasonable when instability persists or sustained reliable pacing is required. Once stabilized, reassess reversibility and permanent-device indications; suspected infranodal disease is a reason for urgent protection, not permission to omit etiological evaluation.')],['US2018','AHAALS2025'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Chronotropic Infusions for Symptomatic Bradycardia',
 'Under the AHA 2025 adult bradycardia-with-a-pulse algorithm, atropine has been ineffective and a monitored infusion is selected while pacing support is prepared. Which pair of starting dose ranges matches the algorithm?',
 'Dopamine 5–20 micrograms/kg/min or epinephrine 2–10 micrograms/min',[
 ('Dopamine 2–5 micrograms/kg/min or epinephrine 2–10 micrograms/min','The epinephrine range matches, but the lower dopamine range resembles low-dose dopamine use rather than the 5–20 micrograms/kg/min specified for bradycardia support. Titration in other settings does not change the named algorithm range.'),
 ('Dopamine 5–20 micrograms/kg/min or epinephrine 0.5–2 micrograms/min','The dopamine range matches, but the epinephrine range is below the 2–10 micrograms/min listed in this algorithm. A cautiously titrated low infusion in an individual setting should not be confused with the requested published range.'),
 ('Dopamine 2–10 micrograms/kg/min or epinephrine 1–5 micrograms/min','These overlapping vasoactive dose ranges may appear in individualized titration, but neither reproduces the pair stated in the AHA bradycardia algorithm. The question tests the named protocol rather than every potentially useful bedside dose.')],
 [S('Algorithm-Specific Doses and Units','**AHA 2025 lists dopamine ==5–20 micrograms/kg/min== or epinephrine ==2–10 micrograms/min== for atropine-unresponsive bradycardia.** Titrate to the clinical response rather than automatically escalating to the upper limit.',
    'Verify concentration, pump programming, patient weight when required and the distinction between milligrams and micrograms. The two dose expressions are not interchangeable.',
    'These are bridging infusion ranges in a named resuscitation algorithm, not a universal prescription for every shock phenotype. The selected agent depends on pressure, rhythm, ischemic risk, available resources and response.'),
  S('Assess Benefit and Adverse Effects','**The target is improved perfusion with an adequate ventricular rate.** Follow blood pressure, mentation, peripheral perfusion, ECG rhythm and ischemic symptoms continuously.',
    'Adrenergic stimulation can provoke tachyarrhythmias and increase myocardial oxygen demand. A faster atrial rate without adequate ventricular conduction or a better circulation is not satisfactory treatment.',
    'Prepare pacing concurrently when advanced block or persistent instability makes it necessary. Treat the cause, including hyperkalemia, ischemia or drug toxicity, because an infusion does not repair the underlying lesion. Reassess dose needs frequently during recovery and taper dopamine gradually as advised by the algorithm. Do not substitute cardiac-arrest boluses or an unverified push-dose preparation for a controlled infusion; formulation and unit errors can cause major harm in a patient with a pulse.')],['AHABRADY2025','AHAALS2025'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Electrical and Mechanical Capture During Transcutaneous Pacing',
 'Transcutaneous pacing is started for unstable complete AV block. The monitor reports 70/min and prominent skeletal muscle contractions occur, but the patient remains poorly perfused. Which finding best establishes effective pacing rather than monitor artefact alone?',
 'Paced ventricular depolarizations with corresponding arterial pressure waves and improved perfusion',[
 ('A pacing spike before each monitor-counted complex, regardless of arterial pressure','Spikes demonstrate stimulus delivery, but the monitor may count pacing artefact or apparent complexes without true ventricular capture. Effective support also requires evidence of resulting circulation.'),
 ('Visible chest-wall contraction at the programmed pacing rate','Skeletal muscle stimulation is common during transcutaneous pacing and contributes to discomfort. It does not demonstrate myocardial depolarization or an effective ventricular stroke volume.'),
 ('A pulse-oximeter numerical pulse rate matching the programmed rate without a reliable waveform','Pulse oximetry can support assessment when its waveform is reliable, but movement and poor perfusion can generate misleading numbers. An isolated displayed rate cannot establish mechanical capture.')],
 [S('Distinguish Stimulus Delivery From Effective Circulation','**Electrical capture means a pacing stimulus produces ventricular depolarization; mechanical capture means that activation produces a circulatory response.** Both must be assessed, because pacing artefact can mimic successful treatment.',
    'Inspect a suitable ECG lead for a consistent paced ventricular response rather than accepting the device-displayed rate. A stimulus spike alone only proves that the generator has delivered an output.',
    'Look for a corresponding pulse and blood-pressure response. A reliable arterial waveform, when already available, provides useful beat-to-beat corroboration. Clinical improvement in mentation and peripheral perfusion strengthens evidence that pacing is effective.'),
  S('Troubleshoot Persistent Poor Perfusion','**Muscle contraction and movement can confound both palpation and monitor signals.** Use concordant electrical and hemodynamic evidence rather than relying on any single artefact-prone sign.',
    'If capture is absent or intermittent, check pad contact and connections, increase output appropriately, reassess pad position and correct hypoxia, acidosis or electrolyte abnormalities. Arrange expert assistance and a more reliable pacing route if needed.',
    'If genuine ventricular capture is present but shock persists, assess ventricular function, preload, ischemia and other causes of low output; increasing the programmed rate alone may not correct them. Continue reassessment after transport, repositioning or sedation because capture can be lost. Provide analgesia and carefully titrated sedation when feasible, while protecting circulation and ventilation. No particular pulse site or ultrasound method should be presented as an infallible capture test; AHA 2025 identifies ongoing limitations in the evidence for optimal confirmation methods.')],['AHAALS2025','US2018'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Temporary Transvenous Pacing for Persistent Unstable AV Block',
 'A patient with complete AV block remains dependent on temporary rate support while a potentially reversible cause is treated. Transcutaneous pacing repeatedly loses capture despite appropriate troubleshooting, and hypotension recurs. Which pacing strategy is most appropriate now?',
 'Arrange urgent temporary transvenous pacing while maintaining available circulatory support',[
 ('Continue transcutaneous pacing alone until the reversible cause has fully resolved','Transcutaneous pacing is a rapid bridge, but repeated loss of capture with recurrent shock makes it unreliable for this patient. Reversibility of the cause does not remove the immediate need for dependable support.'),
 ('Implant a permanent pacemaker immediately because external capture is intermittent','A permanent device can be appropriate for persistent nonreversible disease. Here the definitive indication remains under evaluation, and temporary transvenous pacing can provide reliable support while recovery is assessed.'),
 ('Stop pacing and use an oral chronotropic drug during inpatient observation','Oral chronotropic treatment may have selected specialist uses in other settings, but its delayed and variable effect is unsuitable for recurrent hemodynamic collapse from complete block. The patient requires immediate monitored support.')],
 [S('Indications for an Invasive Temporary Bridge','**Persistent hemodynamically important bradycardia despite medical treatment or unreliable external support can justify temporary transvenous pacing.** The objective is dependable ventricular activation while the cause is corrected or a definitive device is arranged.',
    'The AHA 2025 recommendation supports this approach for persistent unstable bradycardia refractory to medical therapy. Transcutaneous pacing and/or an adrenergic infusion can bridge insertion when needed.',
    'A potentially reversible cause favors reassessing permanent-device need after treatment; it does not justify accepting recurrent shock during that assessment. Conversely, temporary-wire insertion should not become a routine response to stable asymptomatic bradycardia.'),
  S('Procedural Safety and Continuing Assessment','**Temporary transvenous pacing has meaningful procedural and indwelling-lead risks.** Use trained operators, appropriate imaging and monitoring, and the shortest duration consistent with clinical safety.',
    'Complications include lead displacement or loss of capture, ventricular arrhythmias, myocardial perforation or tamponade, vascular injury, thrombosis and infection. Access-specific complications include pneumothorax with some approaches.',
    'Confirm electrical and mechanical capture after placement, establish an appropriate output margin above the measured capture threshold, and reassess sensing, connections and hemodynamics. Keep rescue equipment available. Review the need for the temporary system daily; remove it when safe or proceed to an indicated permanent system when recovery is unlikely. If prolonged temporary support is required, specialist selection of an externalized active-fixation system may improve stability compared with a conventional floating wire. Device selection and timing must also account for infection, anticipated pacing burden and the underlying cardiac disorder.')],['AHAALS2025','US2018','ESC2021'])

R['UKKA2023']=('UK Kidney Association acute hyperkalemia hospital recommendations, 2023','https://guidelines.ukkidney.org/hyperkalaemia/6-summary-of-clinical-practice-guideline-for-hyperkalaemia/6-2-section-ii-hospital/')
R['BRASHCASE']=('BRASH syndrome clinical case report and discussion, 2021','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8091996/')
R['AHATOX2025']=('AHA special circumstances of resuscitation guideline, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001380')
R['UTAHHDI']=('Utah Poison Control high-dose insulin protocol, 2021','https://poisoncontrol.utah.edu/news/2021/07/high-dose-insulin-therapy')

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'BRASH Syndrome and Hyperkalemic Bradycardia',
 'A patient taking atenolol at the prescribed dose develops diarrhea, acute kidney injury, potassium 6.7 mmol/L, a junctional rate of 28/min and BP 72/40 mmHg. There is no overdose history. Atropine has little effect. Which strategy best addresses the immediate mechanism?',
 'Give IV calcium and potassium-shifting treatment, withhold contributory drugs and restore perfusion with monitored support',[
 ('Use pacing as the principal treatment and reassess potassium after ventricular rate normalizes','Pacing may be needed to support circulation, but hyperkalemia and medication synergy continue to impair conduction and can compromise capture. Immediate metabolic treatment must accompany rather than follow rate support.'),
 ('Give glucagon as the principal treatment and defer potassium therapy pending an atenolol level','Glucagon may be considered for beta-blocker poisoning, but this presentation does not require an overdose or a measured drug level to establish a dangerous interaction. Delaying treatment of severe hyperkalemia leaves a major reversible driver untreated.'),
 ('Restore volume with crystalloid alone before deciding whether the bradycardia needs treatment','Diarrheal volume depletion can initiate the cycle and warrants judicious correction. Profound bradycardic shock with severe hyperkalemia nevertheless requires concurrent myocardial protection, potassium treatment and circulatory support.')],
 [S('Renal Dysfunction and AV-Nodal Drug Synergy','**BRASH describes bradycardia, renal failure, AV-nodal blockade, shock and hyperkalemia acting as a reinforcing cycle.** Reduced perfusion worsens kidney function, while potassium retention and drug effects further slow the heart.',
    'Atenolol is renally cleared, so acute kidney injury can increase exposure even without an intentional overdose. Other AV-nodal drugs can contribute through pharmacodynamic synergy; not every implicated agent depends primarily on renal clearance.',
    'The clinical construct has no universally validated diagnostic score. Consider ischemia, isolated poisoning, intrinsic conduction disease and other causes rather than attributing every slow rhythm with kidney dysfunction to BRASH.'),
  S('Interrupt Several Components Together','**With hyperkalemic ECG toxicity, IV calcium protects the myocardium but does not lower potassium.** UKKA 2023 specifies ==30 mL 10% calcium gluconate over 10 minutes== or ==10 mL 10% calcium chloride over 5 minutes== according to clinical setting; reassess the ECG response.',
    'For severe hyperkalemia, the same guideline uses ==10 units soluble insulin with 25 g glucose IV==. Monitor glucose closely for delayed hypoglycemia, particularly in renal dysfunction. Add potassium elimination, including dialysis when required, because intracellular shifting is temporary.',
    'Stop contributory medications, assess volume and restore renal perfusion without indiscriminate fluid loading. Use an appropriate monitored chronotropic or vasopressor bridge and pacing when needed for persistent compromise. Recovery after correction should trigger reassessment before permanent pacing is assumed necessary. BRASH-specific management evidence is mainly observational; the emergency components are supported by established hyperkalemia and bradycardia guidance.')],['UKKA2023','BRASHCASE','AHAALS2025'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'High-Dose Insulin in Cardiotoxic Drug Poisoning',
 'Following a large sustained-release verapamil ingestion, a patient has bradycardia, severe LV systolic depression and persistent shock despite initial calcium and vasopressor support. Toxicology consultation is obtained. Which additional treatment most directly supports toxin-impaired myocardial function?',
 'High-dose IV regular insulin with glucose support and intensive metabolic monitoring',[
 ('Escalate glucagon as the principal additional treatment for verapamil-induced myocardial depression','Glucagon can bypass beta receptors and is considered particularly in beta-blocker poisoning. Its evidence and sustained benefit in calcium-channel-blocker poisoning are limited; high-dose insulin is better supported for the severe cardiogenic phenotype described.'),
 ('Use temporary pacing as the principal additional treatment for myocardial depression','Pacing can assist clinically important bradycardia or AV block, but successful electrical capture may not reverse profound toxin-mediated loss of contractility. Metabolic inotropic support addresses a problem that rate correction alone can leave unresolved.'),
 ('Give IV lipid emulsion as the next preferred treatment before high-dose insulin','Lipid emulsion has a selected rescue role in some lipophilic cardiotoxic poisonings, with stronger established indications in local-anesthetic systemic toxicity. It is not the preferred substitute for high-dose insulin in this verapamil cardiogenic-shock scenario.')],
 [S('Phenotype-Guided Rescue','**Calcium-channel-blocker poisoning can combine bradycardia, impaired contractility and vasodilation.** Non-dihydropyridines such as verapamil particularly impair nodal conduction and myocardial function, although massive overdoses can lose usual selectivity.',
    'High-dose insulin enhances myocardial carbohydrate utilization and provides inotropic support independent of beta-receptor activation. It is useful in severe beta-blocker as well as calcium-channel-blocker cardiotoxicity; it is not simply a glucose-lowering intervention.',
    'Continue appropriate calcium and vasoactive support while assessing ventricular function and vascular tone. Sustained-release exposure can cause prolonged toxicity, so early transient improvement does not establish resolution.'),
  S('Dose and Safety Under Toxicology Supervision','**A published Utah Poison Control protocol uses regular insulin ==1 unit/kg IV bolus== followed initially by ==0.5–1 unit/kg/hour==.** Titrate with specialist guidance to hemodynamic response; escalation protocols vary, so this is not a universal maximum-dose rule.',
    'Provide dextrose according to baseline and serial glucose, using an infusion pump and safeguards against concentration or unit errors. Check glucose about every 15–30 minutes initially and follow potassium, acid–base status and fluid balance closely.',
    'Anticipate hypoglycemia, intracellular potassium shifts and continuing glucose needs after insulin stops. Adjust potassium replacement cautiously rather than treating a redistribution fall as proof of a large total-body deficit. Consider VA-ECMO for selected refractory cardiogenic shock at an experienced center. Recommendations rely substantially on mechanistic evidence, animal work and clinical series rather than definitive large randomized human trials; a favorable mechanism does not justify omitting close bedside reassessment.')],['AHATOX2025','UTAHHDI'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Atrial-Based Pacing for Sinus Node Dysfunction',
 'An active 68-year-old with sinus rhythm has recurrent presyncope during documented sinus pauses despite correction of reversible causes. AV conduction and LVEF are normal. A conventional transvenous pacemaker is planned, venous access is adequate and there is no major comorbidity limiting benefit. Which strategy is generally preferred?',
 'Dual-chamber pacing with programming that preserves appropriate intrinsic ventricular conduction',[
 ('Single-chamber ventricular pacing as the preferred routine system','Ventricular-only pacing can be appropriate with permanent AF, major comorbidity or selected infrequent pacing needs. In an active patient with organized atrial activity and symptomatic SND, it sacrifices useful atrial support and AV coordination.'),
 ('Single-chamber atrial pacing as the preferred routine system','Atrial-only pacing preserves intrinsic ventricular activation and remains an option in selected patients with reliably intact AV conduction. Dual-chamber pacing additionally protects against subsequent AV block and avoids some later reoperations; ESC guidance generally favors it in SND.'),
 ('Dual-chamber pacing programmed to deliver ventricular pacing on every cycle','Dual-chamber hardware is appropriate, but obligate ventricular pacing is unnecessary when intrinsic conduction is normal. Avoidable RV activation can increase dyssynchrony and related adverse outcomes without improving sinus-node support.')],
 [S('Preserve Atrial Support and AV Coordination','**Symptomatic sinus node dysfunction generally favors an atrial-based pacing strategy over ventricular-only pacing.** Atrial stimulation treats inadequate sinus impulse formation while coordinated ventricular timing helps preserve hemodynamics.',
    'Dual-chamber pacing can track or pace organized atrial activity and provide ventricular backup if AV block develops. Rate-adaptive programming is added when clinically relevant chronotropic incompetence is present rather than assumed from a resting pause alone.',
    'Normal AV conduction at implantation does not guarantee lifelong freedom from AV disease. The choice must include future conduction risk, the additional lead burden, venous access and patient preferences.'),
  S('Atrial-Only Versus Dual-Chamber Systems','**The DANPACE comparison supports the usual preference for DDD(R) rather than routine AAIR pacing in SND.** AAIR was associated with more paroxysmal AF and more pacemaker reoperations; the trial did not establish a mortality advantage for DDD(R).',
    'An atrial-only system remains a selected option when its advantages outweigh the risk of later AV block. A ventricular-only system can be reasonable when limited mobility, major competing disease or very infrequent support changes the balance.',
    'After implantation, minimize unnecessary RV pacing while maintaining a clinically appropriate AV interval. An excessively long AV delay can itself impair filling and cause symptoms, so the objective is good physiology rather than the lowest possible ventricular-pacing percentage. Reassess symptoms, atrial arrhythmia burden, capture, sensing and ventricular function as indicated; successful implantation does not remove the need for individualized programming.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Ventricular Pacing in Permanent Atrial Fibrillation',
 'An active patient has permanent AF with no plan to restore sinus rhythm and symptomatic nonreversible slow ventricular responses. LVEF is 60%, no HF or ICD indication exists, and only intermittent ventricular support is anticipated. Which conventional pacing mode best matches these needs?',
 'VVIR pacing with appropriately programmed ventricular rate support',[
 ('DDDR pacing to restore coordinated atrial contraction','Dual-chamber pacing is useful when organized atrial activity can support AV coordination. An atrial lead does not restore effective atrial contraction in established permanent AF and adds hardware without that benefit.'),
 ('AAIR pacing to increase atrial impulse generation','Atrial-only pacing can support selected sinus-node disease with intact AV conduction. It cannot provide dependable ventricular rate support during permanent AF with a slow ventricular response.'),
 ('VVI pacing at a fixed lower rate as the preferred mode for an active patient','Fixed-rate ventricular backup can suit some sedentary patients or limited support requirements. Rate-adaptive ventricular pacing better accommodates activity when the native ventricular response is inadequate, as recommended by ESC guidance for permanent AF requiring pacing.')],
 [S('Match the System to the Available Atrial Rhythm','**Permanent AF removes the usual benefit of an atrial pacing lead for AV synchrony.** Ventricular support is needed when the slow response produces symptoms or another accepted pacing indication is present.',
    'VVIR means ventricular pacing, ventricular sensing, inhibition by sensed ventricular activity and rate modulation. The sensor can raise the pacing rate with activity when the intrinsic response is insufficient.',
    'This differs from paroxysmal AF, where sinus rhythm may recur and a dual-chamber system with appropriate mode switching can remain valuable. Confirm that permanent AF is the intended management state before omitting atrial capability.'),
  S('Separate Pacing Mode From Ventricular Activation Strategy','**A ventricular-only mode does not automatically settle the lead site or device platform.** Expected pacing burden, ventricular function, anatomy and infection risk remain relevant.',
    'The preserved EF and anticipated intermittent support in this question make a straightforward conventional mode choice reasonable. A substantial expected ventricular-pacing burden or impaired LV function would require assessment of physiologic pacing or CRT rather than applying this answer indiscriminately.',
    'Tailor the lower rate and sensor response to symptoms and activity; neither an excessively rapid programmed rate nor an inadequate exercise response is desirable. Review capture thresholds, sensing and the percentage of paced beats during follow-up. Pacing treats bradycardia but does not eliminate AF-related thromboembolic risk, so anticoagulation decisions continue to follow the appropriate AF risk assessment independently of the pacemaker. AV-junction ablation is a different strategy used for selected refractory rapid responses and is not needed merely because AF is permanent.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

R['NBG']=('DICOM standard: NASPE/BPEG generic pacemaker code','https://dicom.nema.org/medical/dicom/2018c/output/chtml/part16/sect_CID_3672.html')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'The Generic Pacemaker Mode Code',
 'In the NBG pacemaker code DDDR, what does the third letter D designate?',
 'A dual response to sensing, incorporating triggered and inhibited functions',[
 ('Pacing capability in both atrium and ventricle','This is the meaning of D in the first position. The third position describes the response to sensed activity rather than which chambers can be stimulated.'),
 ('Sensing capability in both atrium and ventricle','This is the meaning of D in the second position. Sensing capability and the programmed response to sensed events occupy separate positions in the code.'),
 ('Rate modulation in response to metabolic demand','Rate modulation is indicated by R in the fourth position. It allows a sensor-driven pacing rate response and is distinct from the third-position response to sensed electrical activity.')],
 [S('Read the Code by Position','**The same letter has different meanings according to its position.** In DDDR, the first D describes chambers paced, the second D chambers sensed, the third D the response to sensing, and R rate modulation.',
    'A and V identify atrium and ventricle; D in the chamber positions means both. In the response position, I denotes inhibition, T triggering and D both functions.',
    'A sensed intrinsic ventricular event can inhibit an unnecessary ventricular stimulus. A suitably sensed atrial event can initiate AV timing so that ventricular pacing follows if no appropriate intrinsic ventricular event intervenes.'),
  S('Timing Rules Qualify the Shorthand','**DDD does not mean that both chambers are paced on every beat.** Intrinsic activity, refractory periods, upper-rate behavior and other programmed timing rules determine the actual output.',
    'An atrial event outside the relevant tracking window may not trigger a ventricular response. During atrial tachyarrhythmias, automatic mode switching can prevent inappropriate rapid ventricular tracking.',
    'The optional fifth position describes multisite pacing, not defibrillation capability. Mode nomenclature does not establish whether the ventricular lead is apical, septal or part of a conduction-system strategy, and DDDR alone does not imply an ICD. Interpret the surface tracing with interrogation data, lead configuration and programmed intervals. Understanding the code helps distinguish intentional inhibition from output failure and sensor-driven acceleration from tachyarrhythmia; it does not replace a device-specific assessment.')],['NBG','ESC2021'],style='direct',
 table=(['Position','Function','Principal symbols'],[
 ['I','Chambers paced','O none; A atrium; V ventricle; D both'],
 ['II','Chambers sensed','O none; A atrium; V ventricle; D both'],
 ['III','Response to sensing','O none; I inhibited; T triggered; D both'],
 ['IV','Rate modulation','O none; R rate modulation'],
 ['V','Multisite pacing','O none; A atrial; V ventricular; D both']]))

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Rate-Responsive Programming for Chronotropic Incompetence',
 'A patient with a dual-chamber pacemaker for SND remains fatigued during exercise. Interrogation shows normal capture and sensing, DDD mode at 60/min and rate response disabled. Exercise testing reproduces symptoms with an atrial rate of 65/min despite adequate effort; AV conduction is intact and no other limiting disorder is identified. Which adjustment best addresses the finding?',
 'Enable and individually optimize rate-responsive programming with exercise reassessment',[
 ('Increase the fixed lower pacing rate without enabling an activity response','A higher resting lower rate can help selected resting symptoms, but it does not reproduce the changing rate requirements of exercise. Sensor-based adaptation better targets the documented failure to augment rate with activity.'),
 ('Increase only the maximum atrial tracking rate','A higher tracking limit helps when an appropriate atrial rate is being constrained by the upper tracking ceiling. Here atrial rate scarcely rises, so raising that ceiling alone does not provide the missing chronotropic response.'),
 ('Shorten the AV delay to produce consistent ventricular pacing','AV-delay adjustment can address AV timing or selected conduction problems. With intact conduction and inadequate atrial acceleration, forcing ventricular pacing does not correct the principal limitation and can add unnecessary RV activation.')],
 [S('Identify the Rate-Limiting Mechanism','**Chronotropic incompetence is an inadequate heart-rate rise for metabolic demand.** In this patient it is documented during adequate exercise, with normal device function and no competing explanation for the symptoms.',
    'Differentiate an absent atrial-rate response from upper tracking limitation, exercise-induced AV block, loss of capture, ischemia and noncardiac exercise limitation. These mechanisms require different programming or treatment.',
    'A normal resting pulse does not establish adequate exercise support. Medication effects, effort and comorbidity must be considered when interpreting a numerical heart-rate threshold.'),
  S('Optimize the Sensor Response','**Rate-responsive pacing can increase the pacing rate when the native sinus response is insufficient.** ESC 2021 supports DDD with rate response for symptomatic chronotropic incompetence.',
    'Adjust activity threshold, response slope, acceleration and recovery characteristics, and the upper sensor rate according to the device and patient. Reassess during the activities that provoke symptoms rather than accepting nominal settings.',
    'Avoid excessive acceleration with minor movement or an inadequate response during low-motion exercise; different sensor systems behave differently. Preserve appropriate AV timing and minimize unnecessary RV pacing when safe. Benefit is assessed through symptoms and functional response, not simply a larger device-recorded rate. Persistent limitation after optimization requires renewed evaluation rather than repeatedly increasing the sensor gain. Rate-adaptive programming is a treatment for a demonstrated need, not an automatic requirement for every patient with SND or every dual-chamber pacemaker.')],['US2018','ESC2021'])

R['CPP2023']=('HRS/APHRS/LAHRS cardiac physiologic pacing guideline, 2023','https://doi.org/10.1002/joa3.12872')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Minimizing Unnecessary Right Ventricular Pacing',
 'A patient with a dual-chamber pacemaker for SND has normal LV function and reliable intrinsic AV conduction with PR 180 ms. Interrogation shows 96% RV pacing because the programmed AV delay is 120 ms. There is no separate indication for obligatory ventricular pacing. Which adjustment is most appropriate?',
 'Use an appropriate AV interval or ventricular-pacing-minimization algorithm while preserving safe AV timing',[
 ('Retain the short AV delay to maximize ventricular pacing consistency','A short AV interval can be useful when ventricular capture is deliberately required, such as selected resynchronization settings. Here it pre-empts normal conduction and adds avoidable RV pacing without treating the sinus-node problem more effectively.'),
 ('Program a very long AV delay to eliminate nearly every ventricular stimulus','Long AV delays can reduce pacing, but excessive AV separation can impair filling and produce AV dyssynchrony or symptoms. Minimization should not be pursued at the cost of clinically inappropriate timing.'),
 ('Lower the pacing rate below the symptomatic sinus-pause range to reduce pacing overall','Reducing the lower rate can reduce support but risks recurrence of the symptoms for which the system was implanted. The avoidable burden is ventricular pre-emption, which can be addressed without withdrawing necessary atrial support.')],
 [S('Preserve Intrinsic Ventricular Activation When Appropriate','**A short programmed AV delay can trigger RV pacing before an otherwise normal conducted beat arrives.** In SND with intact AV conduction, appropriate programming can preserve native activation while retaining ventricular backup.',
    'ESC 2021 recommends minimizing unnecessary ventricular pacing in patients with SND and a DDD system. Options include suitable AV-delay programming, AV hysteresis and device-specific algorithms that favor conduction.',
    'Check the actual conduction pattern rather than applying an algorithm automatically. Persistent or intermittent advanced AV disease, symptomatic long PR intervals and some resynchronization strategies change the objective.'),
  S('Balance Pacing Burden Against AV Hemodynamics','**A low RV-pacing percentage is not an independent therapeutic goal at any cost.** Excessive AV delay can impair effective atrial contribution and allow undesirable filling patterns.',
    'The ESC SND algorithm cautions against excessively long AV intervals, illustrating avoidance of values above 230 ms. This is a programming caution in context, not a universal physiological threshold for every device or patient.',
    'Review exercise behavior and symptoms after adjustment, because conduction and AV requirements can change with rate. Reassess capture, sensing and the ventricular-pacing percentage using interrogation. If substantial RV pacing cannot safely be avoided, evaluate ventricular function and whether a physiologic pacing strategy is appropriate rather than extending AV timing indefinitely. Monitor for new HF symptoms and pacing-associated ventricular dysfunction; their appearance calls for reassessment of both the underlying disease and the pacing strategy.')],['ESC2021','US2018','CPP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Physiologic Pacing With Substantial Anticipated Ventricular Support',
 'A patient requires permanent pacing for persistent complete AV block. LVEF is 42%, and ventricular pacing is expected on more than 90% of beats. There is no separate ICD indication. Under the HRS/APHRS/LAHRS 2023 framework, which implantation strategy is reasonable to reduce pacing-induced cardiomyopathy risk?',
 'Select cardiac physiologic pacing using an appropriate CRT or conduction-system approach',[
 ('Use conventional RV pacing because preventive physiologic pacing requires LVEF 35% or lower','The familiar 35% threshold applies to important HF device indications, but it does not define all preventive pacing decisions. The 2023 framework supports physiologic pacing with LVEF 36–50% and substantial anticipated ventricular pacing.'),
 ('Use conventional RV pacing and minimize its burden by extending the AV delay','AV-delay programming can preserve native conduction when that conduction is usable. Persistent complete block with more than 90% anticipated support cannot be solved by waiting longer for a conducted ventricular beat.'),
 ('Use conventional RV septal pacing as equivalent to conduction-system capture','RV septal myocardial pacing can provide effective bradycardia support, but the septal location alone does not establish His–Purkinje capture or equivalence to physiologic pacing. The intended activation pattern must be demonstrated rather than inferred from lead position.')],
 [S('Assess Both EF and Expected Pacing Burden','**HRS/APHRS/LAHRS 2023 considers cardiac physiologic pacing reasonable when LVEF is ==36–50%== and substantial ventricular pacing is anticipated.** This addresses prevention of pacing-induced cardiomyopathy rather than only treatment of established wide-QRS HFrEF.',
    'The guideline describes substantial pacing in the approximate range of ==20–40% or greater==. A predicted burden above 90% clearly exceeds that range; the framework should not be misrepresented as a single exact biological cutoff.',
    'Patients with normal EF have a different strength of recommendation, and low anticipated pacing burden changes the balance. Device decisions therefore require more than one EF threshold.'),
  S('Choose and Verify the Physiologic Strategy','**Cardiac physiologic pacing includes CRT and conduction-system pacing.** His-bundle pacing and left-bundle-branch-area pacing are conduction-system approaches; they are not synonymous with conventional RV septal myocardial pacing.',
    'Choose among approaches according to anatomy, operator expertise, capture thresholds, dependence, lead stability, complications and the evidence supporting the relevant clinical setting. The evidence base and technical limitations differ across modalities.',
    'Demonstrate the intended capture pattern and reassess it during follow-up. His-bundle thresholds and the potential need for backup support deserve particular attention in a pacing-dependent patient. Continue ventricular-function surveillance and appropriate medical treatment. Physiologic pacing does not itself establish an ICD indication, and adding defibrillation requires a separate risk assessment. Discuss lead management, battery longevity and procedural trade-offs before implantation rather than presenting every physiologic technique as interchangeable or risk free.')],['CPP2023','US2018'])

R['FDALEADLESS']=('FDA approval: dual-chamber leadless pacing system, 2023','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/cdrh_docs/pdf15/P150035S003A.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Leadless Pacemaker Selection',
 'A patient on hemodialysis has permanent AF and symptomatic nonreversible bradycardia requiring intermittent ventricular support. LVEF is normal. Upper-extremity central venous access is occluded, prior device infection has been fully treated, and there is no active bacteremia. Femoral access is suitable. Which approach most directly addresses the access and pocket concerns?',
 'Consider a leadless ventricular pacemaker after confirming device suitability and pacing requirements',[
 ('Recanalize an upper venous route and implant a conventional pectoral system','Venoplasty or other access techniques can permit a conventional system when its capabilities are needed. Here ventricular-only support is sufficient, and the leadless option directly avoids both the obstructed route and a new pocket in a patient at increased infection risk.'),
 ('Choose surgical epicardial pacing as the preferred initial approach','Epicardial systems can be useful when transvenous or intracardiac options are unsuitable or during cardiac surgery. They require a surgical approach; the described ventricular-only needs and suitable femoral access support considering a less invasive leadless alternative.'),
 ('Use a tunneled transvenous lead and abdominal generator as the preferred initial approach','Tunneled or alternative-site systems can address selected access problems but retain a generator pocket and intravascular lead. They do not offer the same direct avoidance of those concerns as an appropriate intracardiac leadless system.')],
 [S('Access and Infection Risk Guide the Platform','**ESC 2021 supports considering leadless pacing when upper-extremity venous access is absent or pocket-infection risk is particularly high.** Previous infection and hemodialysis are relevant examples, but patient-specific assessment remains essential.',
    'Permanent AF with ventricular-only support needs is a practical setting for a ventricular leadless system. Confirm the anticipated pacing burden and ventricular function before assuming conventional myocardial pacing is the optimal activation strategy.',
    'A leadless device avoids a surgical generator pocket and a conventional transvenous lead. It does not eliminate infection risk or justify implantation during uncontrolled systemic infection.'),
  S('Match Capabilities and Lifetime Management','**Leadless systems are not all limited to the same pacing functions.** Dual-chamber leadless pacing received FDA approval in 2023; device-specific atrial pacing and AV coordination capabilities must be checked rather than inferred from the word leadless.',
    'Some ventricular devices use mechanical atrial sensing to provide AV coordination but cannot directly pace the atrium. That distinction matters in SND and differs from a true dual-chamber leadless system.',
    'Assess femoral anatomy, intracardiac anatomy, perforation and tamponade risk, battery longevity, retrieval feasibility and the plan for future replacement. Conventional CRT and defibrillation requirements need a separate system assessment; a ventricular leadless pacemaker does not supply these functions by itself. Discuss local availability and expertise. The best platform is the one that meets the required physiology and lifetime device needs while addressing the access and infection constraints, rather than the newest device irrespective of indication.')],['ESC2021','FDALEADLESS','CPP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Persistent AV Block After TAVI',
 'Complete AV block begins during TAVI and persists 48 hours later despite correction of electrolytes and withdrawal of contributory drugs. The patient remains dependent on reliable temporary pacing. Under ESC 2021 guidance, which next step is appropriate?',
 'Proceed with permanent pacemaker assessment and implantation for persistent post-TAVI complete block',[
 ('Continue observation to at least day 7 before considering a permanent device','Recovery assessment is important, and observation intervals differ by procedure and conduction pattern. ESC 2021 recommends permanent pacing for complete or high-grade block persisting 24–48 hours after TAVI rather than requiring a universal seven-day wait.'),
 ('Remove temporary support and arrange ambulatory monitoring for delayed recovery','Ambulatory monitoring is useful for selected stable post-TAVI conduction abnormalities without an established pacing indication. Persistent complete block with pacing dependence requires continued protection and a definitive device plan.'),
 ('Perform an EPS and implant only if the HV interval is at least 70 ms','EPS can help risk-stratify uncertain conduction disease, including selected new LBBB after TAVI. Persistent complete block already provides a pacing indication; an additional HV threshold is not required to establish it.')],
 [S('Apply the Procedure-Specific Recommendation','**ESC 2021 recommends permanent pacing for complete or high-grade AV block persisting ==24–48 hours after TAVI==.** Temporary pacing protects the patient while stability, reversibility and the definitive system are assessed.',
    'The timing should not be borrowed from unrelated ICD waiting periods or applied indiscriminately to every postoperative conduction finding. Persistent complete block differs from isolated new LBBB with preserved conduction.',
    'Assess EF, underlying rhythm and expected pacing burden when choosing the device. Near-continuous ventricular support may favor a physiologic pacing strategy in an appropriate patient; the indication for pacing does not settle every implantation detail.'),
  S('Distinguish Other Postprocedural Patterns','**New LBBB alone after TAVI is not an automatic pacemaker indication.** PR and QRS progression, baseline RBBB, transient high-grade block and other risk features guide observation, ambulatory monitoring or EPS.',
    'For selected uncertain cases, continuous ambulatory monitoring for 7–30 days or EPS can be appropriate under the ESC pathway. A positive HV finding is relevant to that uncertainty, not a prerequisite when persistent complete block is already established.',
    'After cardiac surgery, the same guideline generally advises at least five days of observation for high-grade or complete block to assess recovery, with earlier implantation possible when escape is poor or recovery is unlikely. Thus TAVI and surgical waiting rules must remain distinct. Document the conduction trajectory and continue follow-up, because recovered conduction or changing pacing needs can alter programming even after an appropriately indicated implant.')],['ESC2021','CPP2023'])

R['LEADS2026']=('HRS multisociety lead management and extraction consensus update, 2026','https://www.heartrhythmjournal.com/article/S1547-5271%2826%2902264-2/abstract')
R['MDTTROUBLE']=('Medtronic device performance report: pacing troubleshooting','https://wwwp.medtronic.com/productperformance-files/Issue52_MDT_CRDM_PPR_2004_2nd_Edition.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Pacemaker Failure to Capture',
 'Two days after pacemaker implantation, a patient develops presyncope. During a long pause, regularly timed ventricular pacing spikes are visible but are not followed by QRS complexes. Which malfunction is demonstrated by this tracing?',
 'Ventricular failure to capture',[
 ('Ventricular oversensing with pacing inhibition','Oversensing can cause pauses by suppressing needed output in response to noise or another inappropriate signal. Here stimuli continue to appear on schedule, but they fail to produce ventricular depolarization.'),
 ('Ventricular undersensing of intrinsic beats','Undersensing produces inappropriate stimuli because intrinsic ventricular activity is missed, often with spikes appearing despite spontaneous QRS complexes. The described pause instead shows delivered stimuli without a ventricular response.'),
 ('Failure of ventricular stimulus output','Absent output is suspected when expected pacing stimuli are missing, although small bipolar artefacts can be difficult to see. Repeated visible ventricular spikes establish output delivery during the observed noncaptured beats.')],
 [S('Identify the Failed Step','**Capture failure means a delivered pacing stimulus does not depolarize the intended chamber.** For ventricular pacing, a spike without the expected ventricular response is the defining tracing pattern when the stimulus is not simply falling in refractory myocardium.',
    'Distinguish absent output, inappropriate inhibition and failure of myocardial response. An ECG identifies the functional problem but usually does not establish its exact hardware or metabolic cause.',
    'Soon after implantation, lead displacement, connection problems or an inadequate output margin are important possibilities. Perforation is a concern when associated symptoms or imaging findings suggest it.'),
  S('Protect the Patient and Investigate the Cause','**Assess pacing dependence and perfusion before pursuing a detailed device diagnosis.** Presyncope with prolonged pauses requires urgent monitoring, rescue pacing capability and specialist interrogation.',
    'Interrogation evaluates capture threshold, programmed output, sensing, impedance and stored events. Compare lead position with implantation images; a normal radiograph does not exclude microdislodgement. Echocardiography is useful when perforation or effusion is suspected.',
    'Check potassium, acid–base status, ischemia and medications that can increase thresholds. Increasing output can restore capture in some cases while the cause is corrected, but it is not a substitute for treating a displaced or damaged lead. Persistent failure may require lead revision or temporary pacing. Later presentations broaden the differential to lead fracture, insulation failure, fibrosis and generator depletion. Verify both electrical recovery and the resulting circulation after treatment rather than accepting the programmed rate as proof of effective support.')],['MDTTROUBLE','LEADS2026','US2018'],
 table=(['Observed pattern','Principal functional problem'],[
 ['Expected stimulus absent','Output failure or inappropriate inhibition; confirm with interrogation'],
 ['Stimulus without expected depolarization','Failure to capture, after excluding refractory timing'],
 ['Stimuli despite intrinsic activity that should inhibit them','Undersensing, after considering programmed timing'],
 ['Noise sensed with suppression of needed stimuli','Oversensing with inappropriate inhibition']]))

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Ventricular Oversensing and Pacing Inhibition',
 'A pacing-dependent patient has recurrent pauses during shoulder movement. Interrogation records bursts of high-frequency nonphysiological ventricular signals marked as sensed events, followed by inhibition of ventricular output. There is no corresponding ventricular tachyarrhythmia on the surface ECG. Which mechanism best explains the pauses?',
 'Oversensing of electrical noise suppressing needed ventricular pacing',[
 ('Undersensing of spontaneous ventricular depolarizations','Undersensing means intrinsic activity is not recognized and can produce excess or inappropriately timed stimuli. Here the device recognizes extra nonphysiological events and withholds output in response.'),
 ('An increased ventricular capture threshold','A high threshold causes delivered stimuli to fail to depolarize the myocardium. The stored events instead show output being inhibited before a stimulus is delivered, identifying a sensing-related mechanism.'),
 ('Pacemaker-mediated tachycardia using retrograde AV conduction','This is a tracking loop involving ventricular activation, retrograde atrial activation and renewed ventricular pacing in a tracking mode. It produces a paced tachycardia rather than noise-associated suppression of ventricular output.')],
 [S('Recognize Inappropriate Sensed Events','**Oversensing occurs when the device interprets an unwanted signal as cardiac activity.** In a ventricular inhibited mode, that false event can reset timing and suppress a needed stimulus.',
    'Potential sources include lead-related noise, myopotentials, electromagnetic interference, T waves and cross-chamber signals. The stored electrogram and event markers help distinguish these from a true ventricular rhythm.',
    'Movement-associated noise raises concern for lead or connector dysfunction but does not prove a conductor fracture from that observation alone. Review impedance trends, signal amplitudes, imaging and reproducibility with appropriate specialist assessment.'),
  S('Restore Reliable Support','**In a pacing-dependent patient, oversensing can cause syncope or asystolic pauses.** Maintain continuous monitoring and immediate backup pacing while obtaining urgent device expertise.',
    'Specialist reprogramming may temporarily prevent inappropriate inhibition, but increasing the numerical sensitivity setting makes the device less sensitive and can also cause undersensing. A programming change must preserve reliable recognition of genuine cardiac events.',
    'Asynchronous pacing may be used as a supervised bridge in selected circumstances. Magnet behavior is device-specific; an ICD magnet generally suspends tachyarrhythmia therapy without guaranteeing asynchronous bradycardia pacing. Correct the underlying interference or hardware problem rather than relying indefinitely on a workaround. Lead revision, abandonment or extraction requires an individualized assessment of function, infection, venous access and procedural risk. The important distinction is that the pause results from inappropriate suppression of output, not from failure of the myocardium to respond to a delivered stimulus.')],['LEADS2026','MDTTROUBLE','US2018'])

R['BSCSENSING']=('Boston Scientific pacemaker reference guide: sensing and pacing therapies','https://www.bostonscientific.com/content/dam/elabeling/crm/92390662-001_Pacemaker_RG_en_S.pdf')
R['PMTSTUDY']=('Primary study of PVARP extension to distinguish pacemaker-facilitated tachycardias, 2011','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21376834/')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Pacemaker Undersensing',
 'During VVI pacing, spontaneous ventricular complexes outside programmed blanking and refractory periods fail to generate ventricular-sensed markers. Pacing stimuli continue without being reset by these beats. Lead interrogation confirms adequate capture. Which functional abnormality is demonstrated?',
 'Ventricular undersensing',[
 ('Ventricular oversensing','Oversensing means unwanted signals are recognized as cardiac events, typically suppressing needed output in an inhibited mode. Here genuine ventricular events are missed and output continues inappropriately.'),
 ('Ventricular failure to capture','Capture failure means a delivered stimulus fails to produce depolarization when the myocardium is excitable. Capture has been confirmed; the abnormality is failure to recognize intrinsic activity.'),
 ('Normal refractory-period behavior','Events within programmed blanking or refractory periods can be intentionally ignored, producing apparent undersensing. The stem explicitly places the missed beats outside those periods, making normal timing behavior an inadequate explanation.')],
 [S('Separate Sensing From Capture','**Undersensing is failure to recognize intrinsic electrical activity that should be detected.** In an inhibited pacing mode, the device may deliver a stimulus despite a spontaneous beat because that beat did not reset the timing cycle.',
    'A surface tracing alone can suggest the problem, but interrogation is needed to correlate electrograms, markers and programmed timing. Events intentionally excluded by blanking or refractory periods are functional timing phenomena rather than necessarily defective sensing.',
    'Capture and sensing are separate functions. A lead can stimulate myocardium adequately yet record intrinsic signals inadequately, so a satisfactory capture threshold does not prove normal sensing.'),
  S('Interpret Sensitivity Units Correctly','**For conventional fixed sensitivity expressed in millivolts, a lower numerical value means greater sensitivity.** An overly high threshold may miss low-amplitude intrinsic signals; decreasing it can improve detection but can also promote oversensing.',
    'Assess intrinsic electrogram amplitude, lead integrity, connections, position and programmed sensing configuration. Dynamic sensing systems have device-specific behavior, so settings should be interpreted using the relevant manual.',
    'Treat reversible signal changes and correct hardware problems when present. Adjust sensitivity under specialist supervision with confirmation that true beats are detected and noise is not inappropriately counted. Assess pacing dependence and provide rescue capability if the malfunction threatens circulation. Do not confuse sensitivity in mV with stimulation output in volts or pulse width: increasing output treats some capture problems but does not directly correct failure to recognize an intrinsic beat. Recheck device behavior during the circumstances that provoked the abnormality.')],['BSCSENSING','MDTTROUBLE'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Endless-Loop Pacemaker-Mediated Tachycardia',
 'A patient with a DDD pacemaker develops a regular ventricular-paced tachycardia after a PVC. Interrogation shows each paced ventricular beat followed by a retrograde atrial event outside PVARP, which is tracked to the next ventricular stimulus. Which programming intervention most directly interrupts this circuit?',
 'Extend PVARP appropriately or activate the device-specific PMT termination algorithm',[
 ('Increase the maximum tracking rate','A higher tracking ceiling can improve exercise support when normal sinus activity is constrained. In this retrograde tracking loop it does not remove the circuit and may permit a faster paced tachycardia.'),
 ('Increase ventricular stimulus amplitude','Increasing output can correct an inadequate capture margin. Ventricular capture is already present here; the problem is repeated tracking of retrograde atrial activation, not failure of stimulation.'),
 ('Increase the upper sensor rate','The sensor limit governs rate-adaptive pacing in response to activity or other sensor input. This tachycardia is maintained by atrial tracking of retrograde conduction, so changing the sensor ceiling does not directly break the loop.')],
 [S('The Pacemaker Forms the Antegrade Limb','**Classic endless-loop PMT requires a tracking mode and retrograde ventriculoatrial conduction.** A PVC or another disturbance of AV timing can initiate the sequence.',
    'Ventricular activation conducts retrogradely to the atrium. If that atrial event is sensed outside the postventricular atrial refractory period, the device starts an AV interval and delivers another ventricular stimulus, perpetuating the loop.',
    'The rate is often near the upper tracking limit but is also determined by VA conduction and programmed timing. A regular paced rhythm at that limit is a clue, not proof of PMT without electrogram confirmation.'),
  S('Make the Retrograde Event Nontracking','**An appropriately extended PVARP can prevent the retrograde atrial event from being tracked.** Device-specific detection and termination algorithms can accomplish a similar interruption.',
    'Excessive PVARP can restrict tracking during exercise and promote upper-rate behavior. Tailor programming to the measured VA interval, normal activity requirements and the particular system rather than applying a universal interval.',
    'Differentiate sinus or atrial tachycardia tracking, sensor-driven tachycardia and repetitive nonreentrant VA synchrony; these do not share an identical mechanism or programming solution. The primary PVARP-extension study used a simulator and device algorithm testing, so it should not be portrayed as a mortality-outcome trial. For acute symptoms, obtain monitored device expertise; asynchronous programming or a verified pacemaker magnet response can interrupt selected tracking circuits, but an ICD magnet usually does not change bradycardia pacing mode. Persistent hemodynamic instability requires immediate resuscitation appropriate to the actual rhythm.')],['PMTSTUDY','BSCSENSING','ESC2021'])

R['CIEDINFECTION']=('AHA scientific statement on CIED infection, published online 2023','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001187')
R['EHRAINFECTION']=('EHRA international consensus on CIED infection, 2020','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euz246')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Pacemaker Syndrome From AV Dyssynchrony',
 'A patient in sinus rhythm develops exertional weakness, neck pulsations and a fall in systolic pressure during VVI pacing at 70/min. Capture is reliable and LVEF is unchanged. ECG shows retrograde atrial activation after paced QRS complexes. Symptoms improve when coordinated AV activation is restored during supervised testing. What best explains this presentation?',
 'Pacemaker syndrome caused by loss of appropriate AV synchrony',[
 ('Pacing-induced cardiomyopathy','This is ventricular dysfunction associated with a substantial pacing burden after other causes are considered. It can cause HF symptoms, but unchanged EF and an immediate symptom-pressure relationship to AV coordination favor a hemodynamic pacing syndrome here.'),
 ('Endless-loop pacemaker-mediated tachycardia','Classic PMT requires atrial tracking of retrograde activation to perpetuate a paced tachycardia. A VVI system does not provide that atrial tracking limb, and the rhythm here is paced at 70/min rather than a tracking-loop tachycardia.'),
 ('Chronotropic incompetence alone','An inadequate rate response can cause exertional limitation and may coexist. It does not adequately explain retrograde atrial timing, neck pulsations and improved pressure when AV coordination is restored at comparable rate.')],
 [S('AV Timing Can Impair Output Despite Normal Capture','**Pacemaker syndrome is a symptomatic hemodynamic consequence of unfavorable pacing-related AV timing.** It is classically associated with ventricular-only pacing when organized atrial activity remains relevant.',
    'Atrial contraction against closed AV valves can cause prominent venous pulsations and impaired filling. Loss of appropriately timed atrial contribution and reflex vascular effects can reduce pressure and produce fatigue, dizziness or exertional intolerance.',
    'Reliable electrical capture does not establish good hemodynamics. Symptoms can occur without a new reduction in measured LVEF and should not automatically be attributed to lead failure.'),
  S('Confirm the Relationship and Restore Appropriate Coordination','**Correlate symptoms and blood pressure with the pacing mode and atrial timing.** Interrogation, ECG assessment and supervised programming can help distinguish this mechanism from tachyarrhythmia, inadequate rate support and structural deterioration.',
    'If dual-chamber hardware is present, appropriate reprogramming may restore coordination. A ventricular-only system may require an upgrade after individualized assessment of atrial rhythm, access, comorbidity and procedural risk.',
    'ESC guidance favors DDD over ventricular-only pacing in suitable patients with AV block partly to avoid pacemaker syndrome and improve quality of life. This does not imply a universal survival benefit from adding an atrial lead. Permanent AF changes the benefit because useful organized atrial contraction is absent. Optimize AV timing and unnecessary RV pacing after treatment, and investigate residual symptoms for coexisting HF, ischemia or other causes. The diagnosis depends on the clinical hemodynamic relationship, not merely the presence of a ventricular pacing spike.')],['ESC2021','US2018'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Pacemaker Pocket Erosion and Definite Device Infection',
 'A pacemaker generator has eroded through the skin, with a small draining sinus. The patient is afebrile, blood cultures are negative and echocardiography shows no vegetation. Extraction risk is acceptable. Which management is most appropriate?',
 'Arrange complete system removal with appropriate antimicrobial treatment and reassessment of pacing needs',[
 ('Treat with antibiotics while retaining the system because blood cultures are negative','Antibiotics with retained hardware may be used in exceptional patients who cannot undergo extraction, but exposed hardware establishes contamination and definite pocket infection. Negative blood cultures do not make retention the standard curative approach.'),
 ('Remove the generator and retain the transvenous leads because no vegetation is seen','A vegetation need not be visible for the system to be infected. Retained leads can sustain biofilm and relapse, so isolated generator removal is inadequate for definite pocket infection when complete removal is feasible.'),
 ('Revise the pocket and close the skin after local debridement','Pocket revision may address selected noninfectious mechanical problems before exposure. Established erosion with a draining sinus represents infection, and closure over retained contaminated hardware does not provide reliable source control.')],
 [S('Exposed Hardware Establishes the Diagnosis','**Generator erosion, a pocket sinus or purulent drainage constitutes definite pocket infection.** Absence of fever, bacteremia or an echocardiographic vegetation does not exclude this localized form of CIED infection.',
    'Differentiate a superficial early incisional infection confined to skin and subcutaneous tissue without pocket communication. That limited condition can sometimes be treated without extraction; the distinction cannot be extended to exposed generator hardware.',
    'Obtain appropriate blood cultures before antibiotics when clinically feasible, assess systemic involvement and involve an experienced extraction and infection team. Do not postpone necessary antimicrobial treatment in a septic patient.'),
  S('Source Control and Subsequent Pacing','**Definite CIED infection generally requires complete hardware removal plus antimicrobial treatment.** Biofilm on retained components makes antibiotics or local revision alone prone to failure.',
    'Tailor antimicrobial choice and duration to cultures, pocket-only versus systemic disease, valve involvement and extraction findings. An uncomplicated isolated pocket infection after removal commonly receives 10–14 days of treatment; endocarditis requires a longer organism-specific course.',
    'Reassess whether pacing remains indicated and provide a safe temporary strategy if the patient is dependent. Reimplant at a different appropriate site or use another suitable platform only after infection control and the relevant culture-clearance criteria are met. Leadless pacing can reduce pocket concerns but does not replace source control. If extraction risk is prohibitive, specialist suppressive approaches are exceptions requiring explicit individualized assessment, not equivalent curative alternatives for the patient described.')],['CIEDINFECTION','EHRAINFECTION','LEADS2026'])

R['MR2024']=('SCMR consensus on MRI in patients with CIEDs, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jocmr.2024.100995')
R['PROCEDURE2024']=('AHA scientific statement on periprocedural CIED management, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001264')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'MRI With a Non-MR-Conditional Pacemaker',
 'A patient needs clinically important MRI that cannot be adequately replaced by another test. A legacy transvenous pacemaker is not labelled MR conditional, but battery and lead function are satisfactory and there are no abandoned or fractured leads. Which approach is most appropriate?',
 'Refer for individualized assessment and a monitored 1.5-T MRI protocol with device programming and pre/post interrogation',[
 ('Replace the functioning generator before MRI to make the system MR conditional','Conditional status applies to the complete specified system and scanning conditions. Changing the generator alone may leave nonconditional leads or an unapproved combination, while adding procedural risk.'),
 ('Proceed after switching off pacing because the scanner is only 1.5 T','Field strength alone does not provide safety. A pacing-dependent patient may need asynchronous pacing rather than no pacing, and monitoring, trained staff and pre/post assessment remain necessary.'),
 ('Use CT instead because nonconditional labelling makes MRI categorically unsuitable','Alternative imaging is appropriate when it answers the clinical question adequately. Nonconditional labelling is not an absolute ban: selected patients can undergo MRI using an established expert protocol after risk-benefit assessment.')],
 [S('Conditional Labelling Is a System Property','**MR conditional means safety has been demonstrated under specified conditions for a particular system.** Generator, leads, component combinations and scan parameters all matter.',
    'A nonconditional system can still be assessed for MRI in an experienced program. Review the clinical benefit, alternative tests, battery status, lead integrity, implantation history and dependence on pacing before proceeding.',
    'Abandoned, fractured or epicardial leads require additional risk assessment and appropriate expertise. They should not be treated as identical to the straightforward legacy system described here.'),
  S('Protocol-Based Scanning','**SCMR 2024 recommends ==1.5 T== for non-MR-conditional systems.** The protocol includes consent, interrogation, appropriate programming, physiological monitoring and emergency readiness.',
    'Nondependent patients are commonly programmed to OVO or ODO; dependent patients generally require an appropriate asynchronous VOO or DOO mode. ICD tachyarrhythmia therapies must be disabled when relevant, with external rescue arrangements available.',
    'Monitor ECG, pulse oximetry and blood pressure with suitable equipment and personnel, maintaining patient communication. Arrange immediate postscan interrogation, assessment for parameter changes and restoration of the intended settings. A normal pre-scan check does not remove the need for these steps. If the required protocol and staffing cannot be provided, arrange an appropriate referral or alternative investigation rather than performing an unmonitored scan. This evidence supports carefully selected, supervised imaging; it does not establish unrestricted safety for every device configuration or field strength.')],['MR2024','ESC2021'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Electrocautery Precautions in a Pacing-Dependent ICD Patient',
 'A pacing-dependent patient with a transvenous dual-chamber ICD will undergo surgery above the umbilicus with anticipated monopolar electrocautery interference. Which device plan best addresses both pacing inhibition and inappropriate shocks?',
 'Arrange asynchronous pacing programming and suspend ICD tachytherapies, with monitoring and external rescue available',[
 ('Apply an ICD magnet as the sole device intervention','An ICD magnet usually suspends tachyarrhythmia detection or therapy but does not change bradycardia pacing to an asynchronous mode. The dependent patient can therefore remain vulnerable to interference-induced pacing inhibition.'),
 ('Program asynchronous pacing while leaving tachyarrhythmia therapies active','Asynchronous support can protect against inappropriate pacing inhibition. Active ICD detection can still interpret cautery noise as a ventricular tachyarrhythmia and deliver inappropriate therapy.'),
 ('Suspend ICD tachytherapies while retaining the usual inhibited pacing mode','Suspending tachytherapies reduces inappropriate shock risk. Inhibited pacing can still be suppressed by sensed electrocautery noise, which is particularly hazardous when no adequate intrinsic rhythm is available.')],
 [S('Address Two Different Interference Effects','**Electromagnetic interference can inhibit necessary pacing and can trigger inappropriate ICD therapy.** These hazards require separate components of the procedural plan.',
    'Assess the actual underlying rhythm and pacing dependence, device type, programmed settings, surgical site and anticipated current path. A high recorded pacing percentage alone is not a complete test of dependence.',
    'For a dependent transvenous ICD patient with anticipated interference, arrange appropriate asynchronous support and suspension of tachyarrhythmia therapies through the device team.'),
  S('Magnet Response Is Device Specific','**A conventional pacemaker magnet often produces asynchronous pacing, whereas an ICD magnet usually inhibits tachytherapies without changing pacing mode.** Manufacturer, model and programmed response must be verified.',
    'Some devices have absent or programmable magnet responses, and leadless systems differ. Do not infer the response from the presence of a generator alone or assume that a magnet corrects every interference hazard.',
    'Use bipolar electrosurgery when feasible, brief monopolar bursts when required and a return-electrode position that directs current away from the generator and leads. Monitor a reliable pulse or arterial waveform as well as ECG because cautery can obscure the tracing. Have external pacing and defibrillation available while device therapies are altered. Restore the intended settings and confirm ICD therapy reactivation before leaving the monitored care pathway, with interrogation when indicated. Communicate the changes and restoration explicitly between the procedural and device teams; successful surgery does not by itself confirm that protective therapies have been restored.')],['PROCEDURE2024'])

